window.COURSE = [{"id": 1, "unit": 0, "title": "Follow the artifacts", "path": "lessons/0001-follow-the-artifacts.html", "win": "Build a C program and identify which stage produced an error.", "minutes": 15, "search": "Follow the artifacts Build a C program and identify which stage produced an error.  A program is a trail of evidence\nA web framework can hide most of the machine until a bug crosses a boundary. Our starting skill is to locate that boundary. A source file, an object file, an executable file, and a running process are different things. Each is evidence about a different stage. A failed build does not tell you anything about how a process would have run.\n\nCreate a file named hello.c in a scratch directory:\n\nc\ninclude <stdio.h>\nint main(void) {\n    int count = 3;\n    printf(\"records=%d\\n\", count);\n    return 0;\n}\n\n\ninclude makes declarations available. main is the entry function for this hosted C program. Braces delimit its body; a semicolon terminates a statement. printf writes formatted output: %d expects an int. return 0 reports successful termination to the environment. These small contracts matter: the machine does not check that a format string matches the arguments you intended.\n\n Stop the toolchain at each boundary\nRun these commands separately; keep the resulting files:\n\nsh\ncc -std=c17 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -E hello.c -o hello.i\ncc -std=c17 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -S hello.c -o hello.s\ncc -std=c17 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -c hello.c -o hello.o\ncc hello.o -o hello\n./hello\n\n\nThe .i file is preprocessed text; system headers make it much larger than your input. The .s file contains target assembly. The .o file contains machine code and metadata, including references that may remain unresolved. The final command links an executable. The compiler driver coordinates these stages; its name does not mean every error it prints is a compiler error. GCC documents these stopping points explicitly. [[gcc]]\n\nThe output should contain records=3. On a POSIX shell, run echo $? immediately afterward to inspect the exit status. This status is a separate channel from printed text. A program can print a reassuring message and still fail.\n\n A diagnostic experiment\nChange printf to print_records and add void print_records(const char , int); above main. Compile with -c: this should succeed because the call has a declaration. Link the object: it should fail because no definition was supplied. Now remove a semicolon: that fails earlier, while translating source. Restore the working program before continuing.\n\nCompiler messages contain locations, notes, and sometimes suggested repairs. Read the first relevant error before chasing later ones: one malformed declaration can generate a cascade. Warnings are evidence worth investigating, not a proof that a warning-free program is correct. [[warnings]]\n\n::: deep Why the artifact matters\nA source-level change can alter several later artifacts, but the reverse is not unique. Many source expressions can produce the same instructions. A missing header, an unresolved symbol, a loader failure, and a segmentation fault therefore need different investigations. Keep the command, tool version (cc --version), architecture (uname -m), and exact error together. Reproducibility starts before you own a debugger.\n\nOn Linux the executable is normally ELF; on macOS it is normally Mach-O. Apple Silicon typically emits AArch64 assembly. This course labels Linux/x86-64 examples and teaches native C experiments alongside them. Different assembly on your Mac is an observation to explain, not a failed lesson.\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nWithout rerunning the commands, name the input and output of each stage. Which artifact could contain an unresolved external reference? Which command would you use to see expanded headers?\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nPreprocessing transforms C source and included text into expanded source. Compilation transforms that into assembly; assembly produces a relocatable object. Linking combines objects and libraries into an executable. Use -E for expanded headers and -c for an object without a final link. If you only remembered file suffixes, repeat the missing-definition experiment and explain why compilation can succeed.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nA binary exists but launching it reports a missing shared library. Write down why inspecting a semicolon in the source is unlikely to be the fastest first move. In Lab 01, retain an artifact notebook rather than just the final output.\n"}, {"id": 2, "unit": 0, "title": "Read small C functions", "path": "lessons/0002-read-small-c-functions.html", "win": "Trace types, loops, arguments, and return values without guessing.", "minutes": 20, "search": "Read small C functions Trace types, loops, arguments, and return values without guessing.  Start with a complete function\nC gives each expression a type. A type constrains meaningful operations and helps determine the representation used later. Read this function as a state transition, not as punctuation:\n\nc\nint sum_positive(const int values, size_t count) {\n    int total = 0;\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < count; ++i) {\n        if (values[i] > 0) {\n            total += values[i];\n        }\n    }\n    return total;\n}\n\n\nThe calling code must include <stddef.h> for size_t. int is a signed integer type; size_t is an unsigned type used for object sizes and array counts. Do not assume they have the same range. const int values lets the function read integers through this pointer, without assigning to those integers through it. We will unpack pointers next.\n\nThe function has a contract: values must provide access to at least count live integers when count is nonzero, and the mathematical sum must fit in int. A type declaration alone does not encode that contract. The language's type, expression, and statement rules are specified separately; consult §§6.2, 6.5, and 6.8 when a question becomes precise. [[c]]\n\n Execute it on paper\nFor {4, -2, 7} and count = 3, write a table before compiling:\n\n| i before test | i < count | value read | total after body |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 0 | true | 4 | 4 |\n| 1 | true | -2 | 4 |\n| 2 | true | 7 | 11 |\n| 3 | false | no access | 11 |\n\nInitialization happens once. The condition is checked before each body. The increment happens after the body. total += values[i] means add to the current total. == compares; = assigns. A condition treats zero as false and a nonzero value as true.\n\nMake a runnable caller by adding <stdio.h>, defining this function before main, and calling it with an array and its element count. Print the result with %d. Pass count = 0: the loop performs no array access and returns zero. Boundary cases tell you more about an algorithm than a second ordinary input.\n\n Functions pass values\nAn argument initializes a parameter. Changing a local integer parameter does not change the caller's separate integer. Passing a pointer also passes a value: an address-like value that can designate an object. The receiving function may change that designated object if its contract permits. That is why “C passes by reference” is a misleading shortcut.\n\nA declaration announces a name and type; a definition supplies the function body or an object's storage. Headers typically publish declarations. Put one function definition in a .c file and include a header declaring it from its callers. This distinction will become essential at linking time.\n\n::: deep A loop's real preconditions\nFor arbitrary inputs this function is not a safe general-purpose summation API. If the total exceeds INT_MAX, signed overflow violates the language contract. We have stated the precondition rather than silently assuming machine wraparound. Later we will implement checked arithmetic.\n\nTry changing count to int and comparing it to a size_t index. Enable -Wconversion and inspect diagnostics. A warning can expose a dangerous conversion even when your three-element demonstration looks fine. Do not remove a warning by casting until you can explain the mathematical range. [[warnings]]\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nTrace {0, -5, 9, 2} with count = 2. How many reads occur, and what value returns? Then describe the difference between a parameter and the caller's variable.\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nThere are two reads, of 0 and -5; neither is positive, so the result is zero. The remaining elements are outside this call's requested range. A parameter is a separate object initialized from an argument's value. Predicting eleven here means you followed the physical array length instead of the explicit contract.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nChange the task to “return the number of positive values.” Write a new invariant: after iteration i, what does the accumulator mean? An invariant explains a loop for all permitted inputs, including the empty one.\n"}, {"id": 3, "unit": 0, "title": "Pointers have contracts", "path": "lessons/0003-pointers-have-contracts.html", "win": "Draw an array and distinguish an address, a pointed-to value, and a bound.", "minutes": 20, "search": "Pointers have contracts Draw an array and distinguish an address, a pointed-to value, and a bound.  Draw objects before addresses\nAn object is a region of storage with a lifetime and a type. A pointer value can designate an object, but does not carry a universal permission to read whatever numerical address happens to follow it.\n\nc\nint a[4] = {12, 25, 31, 48};\nint p = &a[1];\nint x = p;\np[1] = 99;\n\n\nDraw four adjacent integer boxes. &a[1] produces a pointer to the second element. p reads that element, so x becomes 25. p[1] means (p + 1) and designates the third element of the original array, so it becomes 99. Pointer arithmetic advances by elements, not bytes: if sizeof(int) is 4 on this implementation, adding one advances by four bytes.\n\nThis statement describes the chosen implementation, not a universal C guarantee that every int has four bytes. The pointer and array rules live in §§6.3.2.1 and 6.5.6 of the draft. [[c]]\n\n A bound is part of the interface\nAn array argument usually becomes a pointer to its first element. That conversion loses the array length. Inside void inspect(int a[]), the parameter behaves as a pointer; sizeof a measures that pointer, not the original array. Therefore an API must pass a count, a sentinel with a clear contract, or another representation of the bound.\n\nInside the scope that actually declares int a[4], sizeof a / sizeof a[0] computes the element count. It works because sizeof a still measures the array. Memorizing the formula without its scope condition creates subtle bugs.\n\nA pointer one past the final element may be formed for iteration and comparison within the same array. It must not be dereferenced. Moving still farther is not a valid way to explore nearby memory. An address that looks plausible is insufficient evidence of a valid C access.\n\n Strings add a second length\nchar name[] = \"Ada\"; creates four characters, including a terminating zero byte. The text length is three; the array capacity is four. strlen needs a reachable terminator and does not know the buffer's capacity. A received network byte sequence is not automatically a string.\n\nUse %zu for size_t and %p with an argument converted to void  when printing object pointers. For reading object representation, character access or memcpy is preferable to casting an arbitrary byte address to a pointer with stricter alignment and type requirements.\n\n::: deep Pointer equality is not ownership\nTwo pointers can designate the same live integer. Updating through one changes the value observed through the other. But two similar printed addresses at different times do not prove that the same object is alive: allocators can reuse storage. Keep three facts separate in every pointer diagram: the designated object, the allowed range, and the lifetime.\n\nAddressSanitizer can expose many out-of-bounds accesses during a run. Its success on one input does not establish the bounds of all possible calls. Draw the contract first, then use instrumentation to challenge it. [[asan]]\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nLet q = &a[2] after the earlier writes. What are q[-1], q, and q + 2? Which of these may be read as an integer?\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nq[-1] is a[1], or 25. q is a[2], or 99. q + 2 is one past a[3]; you may form that pointer but may not read an integer through it. Negative indexing is not intrinsically invalid: remaining within the original array matters. Try drawing a new array of length three and redoing the bounds.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nDesign the signature of a function that copies at most n bytes between two non-overlapping buffers. State the source bound, destination capacity, overlap rule, and result. Distinguish each of those facts from a pointer's numerical value.\n"}, {"id": 4, "unit": 0, "title": "Layout and lifetime", "path": "lessons/0004-layout-and-lifetime.html", "win": "Explain why a correctly typed address can still be invalid.", "minutes": 20, "search": "Layout and lifetime Explain why a correctly typed address can still be invalid.  Storage is not a promise of a live object\nA pointer bug is often a time bug. This function has an invalid result contract:\n\nc\nint bad_result(void) {\n    int local[2] = {6, 9};\n    return local; / local stops existing at return /\n}\n\n\nThe pointer may retain a familiar-looking address, but its target's lifetime has ended. A test that “still prints 6” does not rescue the program. Rewrite the interface so the caller provides storage, or allocate storage with explicit ownership and a matching release. The relevant lifetime and storage-duration rules are in §6.2.4. [[c]]\n\nAutomatic storage typically serves ordinary local variables; static storage serves objects that persist for the program's duration; allocated storage persists until released. These language concepts are more reliable than saying “all locals live on the stack”: an optimizer can keep a value in a register or remove it entirely.\n\n Layout adds invisible bytes\nConsider a record used for a file format:\n\nc\ninclude <stddef.h>\nstruct Record { char kind; int value; char active; };\n\n\nOn a common implementation where int has size and alignment four, kind starts at offset 0, value at 4, and active at 8. Tail padding can make the total size 12. Use sizeof(struct Record), _Alignof(struct Record), and offsetof(struct Record, value) to inspect your own implementation.\n\nWhy the gaps? An object's alignment constrains acceptable addresses. Padding helps successive members and successive array elements meet those constraints. Field order can change the size. Do not turn the example's measured offsets into a promise about every compiler, ABI, or packed layout.\n\nWriting the struct's raw bytes to a socket is consequently a poor portable wire format. It leaks padding and ties interpretation to field sizes, byte order, and layout. Later we will serialize fields explicitly.\n\n Ownership makes allocation usable\nA minimal allocation pattern is:\n\nc\ninclude <stdlib.h>\nint make_value(int n) {\n    int p = malloc(sizeof p);\n    if (p != NULL) p = n;\n    return p; / caller owns success and must free it /\n}\n\n\nmalloc supplies suitably aligned storage for ordinary object types and can fail. sizeof p measures the pointed-to type without evaluating a load. A caller checks for NULL, uses the live object, and calls free exactly once. Dereferencing afterward is invalid even if the allocation system has not reused the bytes. [[malloc]]\n\n::: deep Undefined is not random\nUndefined behavior means the language imposes no requirements for that execution. It is not a specified lottery among a few amusing outcomes. Optimizers reason under the assumption that valid executions respect language rules; a lifetime or alignment mistake can therefore change behavior far from the bad line.\n\nKeep three categories distinct: defined behavior you can rely on, implementation-defined behavior the implementation documents, and undefined behavior you must prevent. Instrumentation such as UBSan checks selected violations, not every condition required for a valid program. [[ubsan]]\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nA caller allocates a record, stores its address in two pointers, frees one, and reads through the other. Does having a second pointer keep the object alive? Explain without referring to whether the program crashed.\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nNo. Both values designated the same allocation; neither was a reference count. free ended that allocation's usable lifetime. The second pointer is now dangling. A crash is only one possible symptom. Repair the ownership protocol: exactly one owner releases storage after all permitted borrowers finish.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nUse Lab 01 to measure struct layout and print bytes with unsigned char. Write down which results are guaranteed by the program's contract and which are observations about your current ABI. This habit prevents machine facts from becoming accidental portability assumptions.\n"}, {"id": 5, "unit": 1, "title": "Bytes, bits, and endianness", "path": "lessons/0005-bytes-bits-and-endianness.html", "win": "Decode a byte sequence without confusing bit value with byte order.", "minutes": 20, "search": "Bytes, bits, and endianness Decode a byte sequence without confusing bit value with byte order.  A bit pattern needs an interpretation\nEight bits 11110010 can mean the unsigned integer 242, a signed two's-complement value of -14, or part of an instruction. Storage alone does not carry your intended interpretation. With unsigned width w, bit i contributes bit[i] × 2^i; summing those contributions gives a value from 0 through 2^w - 1.\n\nHexadecimal compresses four bits per digit. 0xB6 is 1011 0110: 11 × 16 + 6 = 182. Group binary from the least significant end when converting. Hex is not a different kind of memory; it is a way to write a value.\n\nThe browser workbench below uses eight-bit bytes and finite widths. These are explicit model assumptions; the C language defines a byte through CHAR_BIT, which is at least eight. Our Linux/macOS lab targets use eight.\n\n Byte order answers a different question\nFor the value 0x0A1B2C3D, a little-endian four-byte encoding places 3D 2C 1B 0A at increasing addresses. A big-endian encoding places 0A 1B 2C 3D. Neither description reverses the order of bits within each byte. x86's architectural documentation specifies little-endian integer storage. [[intel]]\n\nTo decode little-endian bytes b0, b1, b2, b3, compute b0 + 256b1 + 65536b2 + 16777216b3, using a sufficiently wide unsigned type. To encode, repeatedly extract the low eight bits and shift right. This explicit conversion works independently of the host's native byte order.\n\n Masks are small set operations\nFor an unsigned value x, x & 0x0F retains the low four bits. x | mask sets selected bits; x & ~mask clears them; x ^ mask toggles them. x >> k moves bits toward the low end. Shift counts must stay within the promoted operand's width, and signed shifts require additional care. The integer promotions occur before these operations. [[c]]\n\nA packed header could place a three-bit type above a five-bit length. For byte 0xB6, type is 0xB6 >> 5 = 5; length is 0xB6 & 31 = 22. Notice that this is about fields in one value, not about multi-byte endianness.\n\n::: deep Read representations without inventing objects\nYou can inspect an object's bytes through unsigned char , and use memcpy to copy a representation into suitable storage. Casting an arbitrary address to uint32_t  can add alignment and effective-type problems even on a CPU that tolerates some unaligned loads. Conversely, a C access can be invalid before the hardware raises any exception. The language model and hardware model must both be satisfied.\n\nFor an on-disk format, state field width, signedness, byte order, and bounds separately. “It is a 32-bit number” leaves too many unanswered questions.\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nDecode 81 02 as little-endian unsigned 16-bit. Then take the low five bits of 0xB6. Work in hex first and decimal second.\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\n0x0281 = 2 × 256 + 129 = 641. The low-five-bit mask is 0x1F; 0xB6 & 0x1F = 0x16 = 22. If you obtained 0x8102, you assigned the first byte the high significance. If you obtained six for the mask, you kept four bits instead of five.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nInvent a two-byte length field for a message protocol. Write exact encoding and decoding steps, then test values 0, 255, 256, and 65535. Crossing a byte boundary is the useful adversarial case.\n"}, {"id": 6, "unit": 1, "title": "Arithmetic is a contract", "path": "lessons/0006-arithmetic-is-a-contract.html", "win": "Prevent a size calculation from wrapping before allocation.", "minutes": 25, "search": "Arithmetic is a contract Prevent a size calculation from wrapping before allocation.  Width changes arithmetic\nAn unsigned w-bit type computes modulo 2^w. In an eight-bit mathematical model, 250 + 13 = 7 after reduction modulo 256. Signed two's-complement interpretation assigns the top bit weight -2^(w-1). Thus 0xF2, interpreted at eight bits, is -128 + 64 + 32 + 16 + 2 = -14.\n\nDo not transfer the unsigned wrap rule to signed C addition. Under our C17 contract, an unrepresentable signed result is undefined behavior. An ordinary processor instruction may produce low bits, but a C compiler's legal reasoning is not defined by that one observation. Selected signed overflow checks can be instrumented with UBSan. [[ubsan]]\n\n Promotions happen before storage\nConsider unsigned char a = 250, b = 13;. On our target, a + b is evaluated as int, yielding 263; assigning back to unsigned char reduces the value to 7. An eight-bit destination does not imply eight-bit intermediate arithmetic.\n\nLikewise, mixed comparisons can convert a negative integer into an unsigned value. On ordinary targets, -1 < (size_t)1 is false because the converted negative value is large. Do not fix this by guessing at casts. Establish whether negative inputs are permitted, reject them when inappropriate, and compare values in a type that represents the valid domain. Consult the conversion rules in §6.3.1. [[c]]\n\n A real failure: undersized allocation\nSuppose an input supplies an element count and each element needs 24 bytes. This is unsafe:\n\nc\nvoid p = malloc(n  24);\n\n\nIf n is a size_t, the multiplication can wrap to a small allocation. A later loop may still attempt to initialize n elements. The allocation did exactly what it was asked; the arithmetic was wrong before it ran.\n\nUse a division check, with a nonzero element size:\n\nc\ninclude <stdint.h>\ninclude <stdlib.h>\nsize_t item_size = 24;\nif (n > SIZE_MAX / item_size) {\n    / reject the request /\n} else {\n    void p = malloc(n  item_size);\n    / check p, establish ownership, and eventually free /\n}\n\n\nThis is a pattern, not a complete function: define error reporting and zero-length policy for the calling API. calloc performs checked multiplication on the documented Linux implementation; do not substitute an already-overflowed product as one of its arguments. [[malloc]]\n\n::: deep Checked signed addition\nFor int a, b, reject when b > 0 && a > INT_MAX - b, or when b < 0 && a < INT_MIN - b, before evaluating a + b. In these branches the subtractions remain representable. The tempting test a + b < a already performs the dangerous operation and fails for negative values.\n\nA right shift of a negative signed value is implementation-defined in the older C contract used here; a left shift has restrictions beyond the shift count. Prefer unsigned values for bit manipulation and keep signed quantities for their mathematical domain. New language revisions can refine representation rules; the course does not silently assume those revisions.\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nIn a toy unsigned 12-bit size type, the maximum is 4095. An element needs 24 bytes. What is the largest safe count? What allocation size would count 171 produce after modulo reduction?\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nfloor(4095 / 24) = 170, requiring 4080 bytes. Count 171 mathematically needs 4104 bytes, which reduces to eight modulo 4096. An eight-byte allocation cannot hold 171 elements. The tiny model makes visible the same structural bug that appears with much larger real types.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nAudit every allocation and offset calculation in Lab 01. Separate parsing validity, multiplication overflow, addition overflow, and bounds on later indexing; passing one check does not imply the others.\n"}, {"id": 7, "unit": 1, "title": "Floating-point spacing", "path": "lessons/0007-floating-point-spacing.html", "win": "Explain a lost increment using representable spacing rather than decimal folklore.", "minutes": 25, "search": "Floating-point spacing Explain a lost increment using representable spacing rather than decimal folklore.  Representation sets the resolution\nAssume IEEE 754 binary32, the common four-byte float format on our lab targets. A normal value has a sign, an eight-bit biased exponent, and 23 stored fraction bits. The leading binary one is implicit, giving 24 significant bits. The formula is (-1)^s × (1 + fraction/2^23) × 2^(exponent-127).\n\nDerive one value: sign zero, exponent 128, fraction 2^21 gives 1.25 × 2 = 2.5. Do not treat the exponent bits as the exponent directly; subtract the bias. Exponent patterns at the ends encode zeros, subnormals, infinities, and NaNs under this assumed format. Goldberg's paper is the primary deeper reading for rounding and error analysis. [[float]]\n\n An increment can be smaller than the gap\nIn the interval [2^k, 2^(k+1)), adjacent normal binary32 numbers are spaced 2^(k-23) apart. Around 1, the gap above is 2^-23. At 2^24, it is two. Adding one there lands exactly between representable numbers; round-to-nearest, ties-to-even chooses 2^24.\n\nThe arithmetic did not “forget to add.” The exact mathematical result was rounded into the available set. On your compiler, check FLT_RADIX, FLT_MANT_DIG, and FLT_EVAL_METHOD from <float.h> before claiming an expression's intermediates have exactly this behavior. C permits implementation differences beyond the familiar desktop case. [[c]]\n\n Order becomes part of the algorithm\nOn an IEEE binary64 implementation with separate rounding after each operation, compare (1e16 + -1e16) + 1 with 1e16 + (-1e16 + 1). The first yields one; the inner addition in the second rounds away the one, and the outer addition yields zero. A real-valued algebraic identity is not necessarily a floating-point program identity.\n\nWhen summing many values, try pairwise summation or compensated accumulation. Compare to a higher-precision reference on inputs with mixed magnitudes and signs. A smaller measured error on one dataset is evidence for that dataset, not a universal numerical proof.\n\n Comparisons need a domain\nA common comparison checks abs(a-b) <= max(abs_tol, rel_tolmax(abs(a),abs(b))). Absolute tolerance handles values near zero; relative tolerance scales elsewhere. Choose tolerances from the problem's acceptable error and the algorithm's conditioning. NaNs need deliberate handling; infinities should not accidentally pass a subtraction-based test. Exact integer counts and protocol lengths should generally remain integers.\n\n::: deep Cancellation and subnormals\nSubtracting nearby rounded values can expose the error already present in each input. The subtraction is not automatically inaccurate in isolation; the resulting small value can have large relative error compared with the ideal inputs. Track where rounding entered.\n\nSubnormals reduce precision gradually near zero by losing the implicit leading one. Some hardware modes flush them to zero for performance, changing the contract. Fast-math flags may also permit reassociation or assumptions that exclude NaNs. Record compiler flags alongside numeric results, and do not apply such flags as a generic performance switch.\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nFor binary32 in [2^10, 2^11), derive the spacing. Then explain why the spacing doubles at the upper endpoint. Do the powers-of-two algebra without a calculator first.\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nSpacing is 2^(10-23) = 2^-13 = 1/8192. At 2^11, the exponent increases by one while precision stays at 24 significant bits, so spacing becomes 2^-12. The number of significant binary digits is fixed; the absolute gap is not.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nIn Lab 01, compare representations and rounding on your target. Write two assertions: one about the assumed binary32 model and one about the observed C implementation. Identifying the assumption is part of the skill.\n"}, {"id": 8, "unit": 2, "title": "Read registers and addresses", "path": "lessons/0008-read-registers-and-addresses.html", "win": "Translate a short x86-64 instruction sequence into value flow.", "minutes": 20, "search": "Read registers and addresses Translate a short x86-64 instruction sequence into value flow.  Choose a syntax and a target\nThis lesson uses Linux System V AMD64 and AT&T assembly syntax. Source comes before destination, register names start with %, and immediates start with $. Intel syntax reverses the usual operand order and uses different memory notation. Label the syntax before interpreting a snippet.\n\nAn architectural register is named machine state. %rax names a 64-bit register, %eax its low 32 bits. On x86-64, writing %eax clears the upper half of %rax; writing a smaller subregister has different effects. A register's bit pattern does not carry the C type that led to it. The instruction establishes how the bits are used. [[intel]]\n\n Follow values before guessing source\nConsider this illustrative function body for two integer-like inputs:\n\nasm\nleaq 8(%rdi,%rsi,4), %rax\nmovl (%rax), %eax\nret\n\n\nFirst compute rdi + 4rsi + 8 and place that address-like value in %rax. No data load happens during lea. Then load four bytes from the computed address into %eax, clearing the high half of %rax. Finally return. If %rdi points at the beginning of four-byte integers and %rsi holds an index, the address corresponds to element index + 2.\n\nWith base address 0x1000 and index 3, the computed address is 0x1014. If four bytes there encode integer 42 in little-endian form, the returned low 32 bits represent 42. The calculation can be correct while the access is invalid if the original array is too short. Assembly arithmetic does not establish the C bounds contract.\n\n Separate instruction classes\nA move between registers copies bits. A memory load obtains bits from an address. A store modifies addressed memory. Arithmetic computes a new value; comparison updates condition flags used by later instructions. Start an annotation with these categories instead of trying to reconstruct a whole function at once.\n\nThe System V ABI places the first ordinary integer/pointer arguments in %rdi, %rsi, %rdx, %rcx, %r8, %r9; common scalar integer results use %rax. Aggregates, floating-point values, and variadic calls have additional classification rules. Do not extrapolate the simple list to every function signature. [[abi]]\n\n::: deep The same idea on AArch64\nAArch64 commonly passes ordinary integer/pointer arguments in x0 through x7 and returns a simple integer result in x0. Registers w0 through w30 name 32-bit views; writing a w register zeros the upper half of the corresponding x register. Arithmetic and loads are separate instructions in this ISA. Apple platform rules add details beyond the base AAPCS64. [[arm]]\n\nWhen your Apple Silicon compiler emits ldr, add, or madd, translate value flow with the same method. Native output is the right evidence for your program. An x86 snippet in a webpage is a labeled example, not the assembly your machine must emit.\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nFor base 0x2000, index 5, and scale 8, what is 16 + base + index8? Does an LEA-style calculation alone prove the address is readable?\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nThe result is 0x2038: decimal 16 plus 40 gives offset 56, or hexadecimal 0x38. Computing it does not load memory and does not prove mapping, permissions, object lifetime, or array bounds. If you added hexadecimal digits as decimal, convert the offset separately before adding.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nCompile the arithmetic functions in Lab 02 with -S -O0 and -S -O2. Annotate inputs, transformations, memory operations, and result. Preserve multiple plausible C interpretations when the instructions do not distinguish them.\n"}, {"id": 9, "unit": 2, "title": "Recover control flow", "path": "lessons/0009-recover-control-flow.html", "win": "Recover a loop invariant from branches without inventing missing source.", "minutes": 20, "search": "Recover control flow Recover a loop invariant from branches without inventing missing source.  A branch consumes a comparison\nIn AT&T x86 syntax, cmpq %rsi, %rdi sets flags as if %rdi - %rsi had been computed, without storing that difference. je tests equality. jl tests signed less-than; jb tests unsigned below. The same operands can produce different branching decisions depending on the interpretation. [[intel]]\n\nDo not read a conditional jump independently of the instruction that last set the flags. An intervening arithmetic instruction can replace them. Annotate both the comparison and the branch edge.\n\n Build a basic-block graph\nConsider an illustrative loop over 32-bit integers with count in %rsi:\n\nasm\nxorl %eax, %eax\nxorl %ecx, %ecx\n.Ltest:\n    cmpq %rsi, %rcx\n    jae .Ldone\n    addl (%rdi,%rcx,4), %eax\n    incq %rcx\n    jmp .Ltest\n.Ldone:\n    ret\n\n\nThe setup initializes the sum and index to zero. The test exits when the unsigned index is at least the count. The body loads an indexed integer, adds it, increments the index, and returns to the test. A useful invariant is “before the test at index i, the accumulator contains the low 32 bits of the sum of elements before i.”\n\nFor a C interpretation using signed int addition, you must additionally constrain inputs so the sum is representable. Machine-level low-bit addition alone cannot tell you whether the original source was signed, unsigned, or produced under a special overflow flag.\n\n Source structure is not preserved\nA compiler may rotate a loop so the test is at the bottom, replace a branch with a conditional move, unroll several iterations, or eliminate an entire computation. Find the entry edge, back edge, and exit edge. Then ask which values change at each edge. Do not insist on seeing a familiar for layout.\n\nA conditional move can remove a branch penalty but may calculate both candidate values. Whether it is beneficial depends on data dependencies, instruction costs, and predictability. Compiler optimization decisions are target- and version-dependent. [[opt]]\n\n::: deep Signedness leaves clues, not complete types\nThe use of jb suggests an unsigned comparison at that point; it does not prove the original variable had a particular typedef. Compilers can transform comparisons while preserving all valid source behaviors. Similarly, a test against zero may combine several source conditions.\n\nTo recover a function, generate candidate source, compile it under matching flags, and compare the value flow. Equivalent behavior is a stronger goal than identical instruction spelling. Optimization, compiler version, and target features can all change the spelling while preserving the contract.\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nTrace the loop with count zero. Then trace count three and elements {5, 7, 2}. Which instruction first prevents the zero-count memory access?\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nAt count zero, index zero satisfies index >= count; jae jumps directly to return and the result remains zero. For count three, the successive sums are 5, 12, and 14. The comparison and jae edge guard the load. A loop written with an unconditional first load would need a separate empty-input guard.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nCompile a signed minimum and an unsigned minimum in Lab 02. Predict the result for the same high-bit input pattern before inspecting assembly. Explain the interpretation rather than saying one conditional jump is simply “less than.”\n"}, {"id": 10, "unit": 2, "title": "Calls, stacks, and register ownership", "path": "lessons/0010-calls-stacks-and-ownership.html", "win": "Explain what must survive a function call and who preserves it.", "minutes": 25, "search": "Calls, stacks, and register ownership Explain what must survive a function call and who preserves it.  A call is an agreement\nA function boundary crosses separately compiled code. The ABI specifies where arguments and results live, which registers the callee must preserve, and how the stack is aligned. A function may have correct arithmetic and still break its caller by violating that agreement.\n\nUnder System V AMD64, %rbx, %rbp, and %r12–%r15 are preserved by the callee. Many other ordinary registers are caller-saved: a caller that needs their values after a call must arrange preservation itself. %rsp must also be restored appropriately. The exact rules include more detail for vector registers and complex signatures. [[abi]]\n\n Trace a nested call\nSuppose outer(x) needs x after calling inner(x). Keeping x only in %rdi is insufficient because inner may overwrite it. The compiler can spill x to the stack, copy it into a callee-saved register after preserving that register's original value, or recompute it if possible.\n\nA conventional x86 call stores a return address on the stack and transfers control. ret obtains the saved return address and continues there. The stack is also used for spills, some arguments, saved registers, and addressable locals. It is not merely a pile of C variables.\n\nAt ordinary System V AMD64 function entry, %rsp + 8 has the required 16-byte alignment because the call pushed eight bytes. A call-site convention and an entry convention are easy to confuse. The ABI also defines a red zone for particular uses; signal and kernel contexts have different constraints. Do not build handwritten assembly from a simplified drawing alone.\n\n Stack frames are a debugging aid\nAt low optimization, a frame pointer often makes the call chain and locals easy to inspect. Optimized code can omit it, inline functions, or perform tail calls. Unwinding may use metadata instead. A missing source frame in a debugger does not automatically mean the stack is corrupt.\n\nUse backtrace, frame, info registers, and disassembly to align three views: the source call, the instruction pointer, and the stack's saved state. GDB documents both source and machine-level examination. [[gdb]]\n\n::: deep AArch64 changes the return mechanism\nAArch64 commonly places the return address in link register x30 rather than pushing it automatically. A function making another call must preserve its own return address somewhere suitable, often its stack frame. The stack pointer has alignment requirements, and the AAPCS64 defines a different preserved-register set. [[arm]]\n\nThis comparison reveals the stable idea: a return path and caller state must survive nested calls. The particular register names and automatic stack action are ISA/ABI details. It also explains why copying an x86 stack diagram directly onto Apple Silicon is misleading.\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nA caller stores a needed temporary in a caller-saved register, invokes an unknown ABI-conforming function, and uses the temporary afterward. Who was responsible for preserving it? Can a successful run prove the code is safe for every callee?\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nThe caller was responsible. The particular callee in one run may happen to leave the register unchanged, but another valid implementation may clobber it. Correctness depends on the ABI contract, not a lucky observation. If the callee instead damages a callee-saved register without restoration, the callee is at fault.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nIn Lab 02, follow a nested function call at two optimization levels. Identify one value that must survive, where it lives across the call, and which rule explains that choice. Then compare with the native assembly from your own machine.\n"}, {"id": 11, "unit": 2, "title": "Recover data layout", "path": "lessons/0011-recover-data-layout.html", "win": "Infer candidate array and record layouts from address calculations.", "minutes": 20, "search": "Recover data layout Infer candidate array and record layouts from address calculations.  Addresses expose structure\nFor a genuine contiguous C array int a[rows][7], each row contains seven integers. On our four-byte-int target, a[i][j] has byte offset (i7 + j)4. The row stride is 28 bytes; the column stride is four.\n\nA compiler might compute i7 as a shift and subtraction, or combine address calculations in another way. An instruction's scale factor need not spell the source dimension directly. Reduce the arithmetic first, then propose a data layout.\n\nA pointer-to-pointer representation is different: int p commonly requires loading a row pointer before locating an element. Those rows need not be adjacent. A contiguous 2D array does not decay into int ; the row type matters. The array and pointer rules in the language draft explain the distinction. [[c]]\n\n Field offsets constrain hypotheses\nSuppose a function loads one byte from offset 0 and a four-byte value from offset 8 of the same base pointer. That is evidence for two fields or accesses at those offsets. It does not uniquely reveal member names, padding, or the full record size.\n\nWrite a candidate struct and inspect it with offsetof and sizeof. If your candidate places the second field at offset 4, it does not explain the observed offset 8 without an additional assumption. Debug information can reveal declared types when available, but stripped machine code often leaves several candidates.\n\nA structure's alignment and aggregate passing rules also affect function boundaries. Small structs may travel through registers; larger ones may use memory or hidden pointers. Use the target ABI rather than the assumption “a struct argument is always copied on the stack.” [[abi]]\n\n Turn reverse engineering into a falsifiable claim\nStart with a statement such as “this address equals base plus 28i plus 4j.” Then list candidate interpretations: a seven-column integer array, a byte buffer with equivalent strides, or part of a larger packed layout. Choose an input that distinguishes them when you control execution. A hypothesis that explains one input but not the stride over many rows is incomplete.\n\nObject-file metadata can help: symbol size, alignment, and section placement constrain possibilities. readelf exposes ELF metadata, but does not reconstruct absent source names by magic. [[readelf]]\n\n::: deep Padding changes arrays of records\nIf a record's fields occupy 13 bytes but its alignment is eight, its size may round to 16. Then adjacent records start 16 bytes apart. A load with stride 16 may reflect tail padding, not a hidden four-byte field. Reorder fields experimentally, recompile, and compare both sizeof and generated addressing.\n\nFor a portable wire format, avoid guessing a native struct declaration that happens to match one capture. Decode explicit byte fields. Matching one ABI's padding is not a protocol specification.\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nAn access uses base + i40 + j8. Under the hypothesis of a contiguous matrix of eight-byte elements, how many columns are there? What other evidence would you seek before asserting that hypothesis as fact?\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nFive columns: the row stride is 40 bytes and each element occupies eight. Seek loop bounds, allocation size, adjacent access patterns, debug types, and caller contracts. Those may support or disprove the matrix interpretation. The address arithmetic alone also fits other layouts.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nCreate two logically equivalent data layouts in Lab 02: an array of records and separate arrays per field. Compare load addresses and later revisit the same choice when studying cache locality.\n"}, {"id": 12, "unit": 2, "title": "Debug the first bad write", "path": "lessons/0012-debug-the-first-bad-write.html", "win": "Distinguish the observed crash from the earlier operation that caused it.", "minutes": 25, "search": "Debug the first bad write Distinguish the observed crash from the earlier operation that caused it.  The faulting line is a witness\nA crash tells you where execution encountered a problem it could not continue through. It does not necessarily identify the original mistake. A buffer overrun can damage a neighboring pointer; much later, an innocent-looking dereference faults.\n\nUse a small input that reproduces the symptom. Record the exact compiler flags, executable, input, and command. Rebuild with debug information and a manageable optimization level, then inspect the failing state. Keep the original optimized reproducer too: timing, layout, and undefined behavior can change with instrumentation.\n\n Ask the debugger a question\nFor Linux with GDB, a useful sequence is:\n\ntext\ngdb ./program\nbreak main\nrun\nbacktrace\nframe 0\ninfo locals\ninfo registers\nx/16bx address\n\n\nReplace address with an actual object address obtained from the current frame. x/16bx examines sixteen bytes in hex. next steps over source calls; step enters them; stepi advances an instruction. A watchpoint on a live object can stop when its value changes, helping identify the first unexpected writer. Hardware resources constrain watchpoint sizes and counts. [[gdb]]\n\nOn macOS use LLDB's breakpoint set, run, bt, frame variable, register read, and memory read; the official command map translates workflows. Attaching or launching may require local debugger permissions that a hosted environment lacks. [[lldb]]\n\n Instrument a hypothesis\nBuild the Lab 02 memory example with AddressSanitizer. The report distinguishes an access site, the accessed address's relationship to an allocation, and often allocation or release stacks. Read all three. “Heap-use-after-free” is an explanation of a violated lifetime, not just an intimidating label. [[asan]]\n\nRepair the cause in source and rerun both the reproducer and boundary inputs. A successful run with the old input is necessary evidence, but not the whole repair: explain which invariant now prevents the class of violation. Examples include an explicit capacity check, a corrected ownership transfer, or removal of an invalid returned pointer.\n\n::: deep Readable memory is not valid C storage\nA debugger can show bytes at a dead object's former address. The operating system may still map the page, and the bytes may look exactly as expected. Neither fact restores object lifetime. Similarly, writing outside a small array can stay inside a mapped page and avoid an immediate hardware fault.\n\nSanitizers add a layer that catches some language-level violations invisible to page protection. They have runtime and platform limitations, and only inspect exercised paths. Combine a concrete report with a reasoned contract; do not equate silence with proof.\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nYour program faults while reading node->next. List three earlier causes that could produce this symptom and one observation that would distinguish each.\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nThe node may have been freed: inspect release history or an ASan use-after-free report. The pointer may have been overwritten: a watchpoint or nearby bounds report can identify a writer. The object may never have been initialized: trace construction and assignments, possibly using an appropriate uninitialized-memory tool. A null value is another possibility, but it does not explain why it became null. The exercise is to move from symptom to testable history.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nProduce a short investigation note for Lab 02: symptom, violated invariant, earliest demonstrated bad operation, repair, and evidence against a competing hypothesis. This note is more valuable than a screenshot of a clean exit.\n"}, {"id": 13, "unit": 3, "title": "Symbols and relocations", "path": "lessons/0013-symbols-and-relocations.html", "win": "Explain how separately compiled references acquire final addresses.", "minutes": 25, "search": "Symbols and relocations Explain how separately compiled references acquire final addresses.  Two jobs at one boundary\nA linker resolves names and adjusts references. Symbol resolution answers “which definition satisfies this reference?” Relocation answers “what value or displacement belongs at this site after placement?” Neither requires the linker to reconstruct your original C program.\n\nMake api.h declare int adjust(int);, define adjust in adjust.c, and call it from main.c. Compile the two source files independently with -c, then link the objects together. The calling object can contain machine instructions even while the callee's final location is unknown. GCC's stage controls let you retain that intermediate artifact. [[gcc]]\n\n Inspect the evidence on Linux\nUse these commands with your own files:\n\nsh\nreadelf -s main.o\nreadelf -r main.o\nreadelf -S main.o\nobjdump -dr main.o\ncc main.o adjust.o -o demo\nreadelf -l demo\n\n\nThe symbol table can identify an undefined external name. Relocation entries identify places that depend on symbol placement. Section headers describe linker-oriented divisions such as code and data. Program headers describe loadable segments and other runtime information. Sections and segments answer different questions; a source variable does not correspond one-to-one to a segment. [[readelf]]\n\nThe exact relocation kinds depend on architecture, position independence, and compiler flags. Describe the role of an entry before memorizing its target-specific name.\n\n Linkage is a source-level design choice\nA file-scope helper declared static has internal linkage; it is not the same helper name in every translation unit. An extern declaration normally refers to an externally linked definition. Put declarations in a header; place one intended definition in a .c file. Including a header that defines an ordinary global in several translation units can produce multiple-definition failures.\n\nA clean repair uses the intended ownership of the symbol, rather than a linker option that suppresses conflicting definitions. When the same name has incompatible declarations across separately compiled files, a successful link need not establish a sound type contract.\n\n::: deep Position independence still requires coordination\nA position-independent executable can be loaded at varying bases, supporting address-space randomization. Many references use relative addressing, but some still require tables or loader work. “Position independent” does not mean “there are no relocations.”\n\nOn macOS, the object/executable format is Mach-O. otool, nm, and the platform linker provide related evidence with different metadata. Lab 02's C build is portable; its ELF inspection instructions are explicitly a Linux track. Use a Linux VM/container when you need those exact artifacts.\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nCompilation succeeds for both files; the final link reports an undefined reference to adjust. Name three concrete causes and the artifact you would inspect first.\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nThe defining object may be missing from the command, the definition may have internal linkage, or its exported name may differ because of a typo or language linkage. Inspect the object symbol tables and the actual link command. Repeating the compile does not address a missing link input. The GNU linker manual explains command-line input processing. [[ld]]\n:::\n\n Transfer\nDeliberately produce one unresolved reference and one multiple-definition error in scratch copies. Write a different diagnosis and minimal source/build repair for each. Preserve the working lab files.\n"}, {"id": 14, "unit": 3, "title": "Libraries and the loader", "path": "lessons/0014-libraries-and-the-loader.html", "win": "Separate link-time library selection from runtime library discovery.", "minutes": 25, "search": "Libraries and the loader Separate link-time library selection from runtime library discovery.  Static and shared are different promises\nA static archive is a collection of object files. A traditional linker selects archive members needed to satisfy unresolved references at the point where it processes that archive. Input order can therefore matter: objects that need functions typically come before archives that provide them. Linker grouping options exist for circular dependencies, but redesigning the dependency is often clearer. [[ld]]\n\nA shared library is loaded into the process at runtime rather than copied into every executable in the same way. The executable records dependencies and enough metadata for the loader to complete the runtime arrangement. Build success proves that the build environment satisfied its part of the contract, not that a different machine will.\n\n An executable is not a running process\nOn Linux, launching a dynamic ELF executable involves the kernel and its designated interpreter/loader. The loader locates shared objects, maps them, performs required relocations, and arranges initialization before ordinary application execution. The ld.so manual documents search paths and diagnostic environment settings. [[loader]]\n\nFor a binary you built yourself, try LD_DEBUG=libs ./demo on a suitable glibc Linux system. Observe which directories and filenames are considered. This is a diagnostic for a known program, not a guarantee that every Linux libc uses the same diagnostics. macOS uses its own loader and dyld conventions.\n\nAvoid solving a deployment problem by copying an arbitrary library with the same filename. ABI compatibility includes symbol availability and versioning, layouts, calling conventions, and assumptions made by both sides. A filename match is weak evidence.\n\n Observe mappings without overclaiming\nA Linux process's /proc/PID/maps lists virtual ranges, permissions, offsets, and mapped files. Compare an executable's mappings with a shared object's mappings. Read-only code pages may be shareable across processes, while writable data and private copies behave differently. The mapping listing alone does not prove a page is resident in RAM or unique to that process. [[maps]]\n\nAddress-space layout randomization means absolute addresses may change between runs. Compare relationships and symbol-relative offsets instead of assuming a debugger address is a stable identifier for future executions.\n\n::: deep Lazy work and indirection\nA procedure linkage table and global offset table are part of common ELF mechanisms for indirect dynamic references. Some configurations resolve calls lazily; others resolve eagerly. Security settings, toolchain options, and loader implementations alter the details. The useful invariant is that an external reference needs a well-defined target at the moment it is used.\n\nDistinguish symbol interposition from static lexical scope: a loader can sometimes select among external definitions in ways that surprise someone reasoning only from source file order. Keep interfaces narrow and avoid relying on accidental process-global symbol collisions.\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nYou move a working binary into a minimal Linux image. It now fails before printing from main. List the evidence you would collect before editing the application logic.\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nCollect the exact launch error, executable format/architecture, ELF interpreter path, required shared objects, and the runtime's available loader/libraries. Use readelf on the known binary and loader diagnostics where applicable. Missing interpreter, wrong architecture, and missing dependency are different hypotheses. A main breakpoint that never triggers supports a pre-application failure, but does not by itself identify which one.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nWrite a deployment contract for your future capstone: architecture, OS interface, runtime library requirements, and invocation. Explain which properties a static build simplifies and which it does not remove, such as kernel interface and instruction-set requirements.\n"}, {"id": 15, "unit": 4, "title": "A pipeline is a model", "path": "lessons/0015-a-pipeline-is-a-model.html", "win": "Use a pipeline timing model and identify which details it deliberately omits.", "minutes": 25, "search": "A pipeline is a model Use a pipeline timing model and identify which details it deliberately omits.  Build a machine small enough to reason about\nAn instruction-set architecture specifies observable machine behavior. A microarchitecture implements it. Two processors can execute the same instruction set with different pipelines, caches, execution widths, and timings. Never turn a simple teaching pipeline into a literal schematic of every modern CPU. Intel's manuals separate architectural behavior from processor-specific optimization guidance. [[intel]]\n\nFor a first timing model, give each instruction five stages: fetch, decode, execute, memory, and writeback. Assume one cycle per stage and one new instruction entering per cycle. Without overlap, eight instructions take 40 stage-cycles of elapsed time. With ideal overlap, the first finishes at cycle five and the eighth at cycle twelve.\n\nLatency is the time for one instruction to pass through. Throughput is the completion rate once overlap is established. Pipelining improves the latter without necessarily reducing the former. Clock frequency alone does not tell you either program runtime or useful work per second.\n\n Derive where the model fails\nA data hazard occurs when an instruction needs a result not yet available. Forwarding may supply it before writeback, but a load's value still depends on memory. A structural hazard occurs when two operations need a resource the model cannot serve simultaneously. A control hazard occurs when the next instruction address depends on an unresolved branch.\n\nTake load a; add a, b; store b. The add depends on the load, and the store depends on the add. If the memory access takes longer, simply drawing more overlap does not create the missing value. Add a stall or a different execution mechanism to your model, then recompute the timeline.\n\n Combinational work and saved state\nA datapath contains computations such as adders and selectors, plus state such as registers. Combinational logic maps current inputs to outputs; state elements preserve values across clock boundaries. A clock period must accommodate relevant propagation and state-transition requirements. Splitting work into more stages can shorten some paths but adds pipeline overhead and complicates hazards.\n\nThis explains why “double the stages” is not a universal “double the performance” rule. Branch recovery, forwarding, power, and implementation complexity interact. Programmer-visible instruction count is also not necessarily the internal operation count.\n\n::: deep Modern execution preserves a simpler appearance\nAn out-of-order processor may issue ready operations before older stalled operations, rename registers to remove false dependencies, and predict branches. It still needs to preserve architectural rules for completed execution, including exceptions. Speculative work can be discarded architecturally while affecting caches and other microarchitectural state.\n\nYou do not need a transistor-level model to debug a dependency-bound loop. You do need to identify the assumptions behind your timing claim. “One cycle per source line” and “all independent operations are free” are both unsupported models. Compiler transformations further change the relationship between source and instructions. [[opt]]\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nFor an ideal six-stage pipeline and ten instructions, derive elapsed cycles, steady-state throughput, and the latency of one instruction. Then say which result changes if every instruction depends on an unavailable previous result.\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nElapsed time is 6 + 10 - 1 = 15 cycles, throughput after filling is one instruction per cycle, and single-instruction latency is six cycles. Dependencies can force stalls and reduce realized throughput, invalidating the fifteen-cycle prediction. The baseline six-stage latency remains the model's latency absent those extra stalls.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nDraw a seven-instruction timeline containing a taken branch and a two-cycle extra load delay. State a branch-resolution rule and a forwarding rule before calculating. Two different answers can both be correct if their explicitly stated models differ.\n"}, {"id": 16, "unit": 4, "title": "Dependencies limit throughput", "path": "lessons/0016-dependencies-limit-throughput.html", "win": "Explain why multiple accumulators can outperform an unrolled dependency chain.", "minutes": 25, "search": "Dependencies limit throughput Explain why multiple accumulators can outperform an unrolled dependency chain.  Count chains, not just instructions\nConsider a sum s += a[i]. Every update reads the preceding value of s. If an addition has latency three cycles on a chosen machine, that chain cannot start dependent additions every cycle merely because several add units exist. This is a latency-bound recurrence.\n\nUnrolling four iterations into four consecutive updates to the same s reduces loop overhead but retains the chain. Using four independent accumulators creates four shorter chains and combines them at the end. The change can expose instruction-level parallelism; its benefit depends on loads, execution resources, register pressure, and the final reduction.\n\n Distinguish true and false dependencies\nA read-after-write dependency carries a value required by the computation. Register renaming cannot remove it. Reusing an architectural register name for unrelated values can create write-after-write or write-after-read naming conflicts; a renaming implementation may eliminate those conflicts internally.\n\nCompilers also face aliasing uncertainty. If a store through one pointer may change a value read through another, moving the read across the store may change valid program behavior. restrict can give a useful non-aliasing contract, but violating that promise makes the source invalid. It is an obligation on callers, not a magic speed annotation.\n\n SIMD needs compatible work\nA vector instruction can operate on several lanes. This is different from overlapping independent scalar instructions, although both can coexist. A loop with independent elementwise operations is a better candidate than a loop where each element depends on the previous result.\n\nCompile a simple array transformation with GCC's vectorization reports (-fopt-info-vec-optimized and -fopt-info-vec-missed on supported GCC versions). For Clang, use its corresponding optimization remarks rather than passing GCC-only options blindly. Look at assembly and measured behavior, not just whether a report says “vectorized.” [[opt]]\n\n Preserve the algorithm's numerical contract\nFor unsigned modular addition, reassociating a sum preserves the modular result. For floating-point addition, changing grouping can change the rounded answer. A multi-accumulator sum may therefore be a different numerical algorithm. Choose a correctness tolerance based on the task before judging the speedup. Goldberg's analysis explains why real-number algebra is insufficient. [[float]]\n\n::: deep Bounds for a first performance model\nSuppose a toy machine can execute two additions per cycle, but each addition's result takes three cycles. One independent recurrence achieves at best one addition every three cycles. Three independent recurrences can in principle hide the latency up to one addition per cycle; six may be needed to reach two per cycle, ignoring all other resources.\n\nThese are model bounds, not promises for your CPU. Loads may dominate; instruction decoding may constrain issue; more accumulators may spill registers. Use model predictions to choose measurements. Processor optimization manuals supply target-specific constraints, while your compiled binary supplies the actual instruction stream. [[intel]]\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nDoes replacing one accumulator with four guarantee a fourfold improvement? Name two conditions that can prevent it and one condition under which the transformation can alter the result.\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nMemory bandwidth, issue limits, register spills, and loop overhead can all constrain the gain. Floating-point reassociation can change results. If your explanation is only “the CPU is faster,” identify a resource or dependency and predict a measurable consequence, such as fewer cycles with similar bytes transferred.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nIn Lab 03, first compare traversal orders with identical sums. As an extension, add independent unsigned accumulators. Report whether your data supports a latency, throughput, or memory explanation. Keep the extension separate from the baseline so two changes do not confound each other.\n"}, {"id": 17, "unit": 4, "title": "Measure a falsifiable claim", "path": "lessons/0017-measure-a-falsifiable-claim.html", "win": "Design a benchmark that can disprove your explanation of a speedup.", "minutes": 25, "search": "Measure a falsifiable claim Design a benchmark that can disprove your explanation of a speedup.  Begin with a question\n“Make it faster” is not an experiment. “Changing traversal order reduces elapsed time by reducing cache misses for this matrix size” is a hypothesis with a mechanism, workload, and observable outcomes. It might be false. That is what makes it useful.\n\nMeasure elapsed time with a monotonic clock, repeat measurements, retain raw samples, and report the workload and environment. Keep correctness checks outside the timed region where practical. Prevent the optimizer from eliminating the work by consuming and checking its result; do not blanket the program in volatile and accidentally benchmark a different problem.\n\n Separate useful metrics\nElapsed time includes waiting and scheduling. CPU time counts time charged to execution under the platform's accounting rules. Instructions retired measure one aspect of work; cycles reflect time spent while a counter runs. Instructions per cycle is a ratio, not a universal efficiency grade. A memory-bound program can have low IPC for a real reason.\n\nOn Linux, begin with perf stat ./program when permissions and hardware support permit. Add selected counters only when they answer a question. Events and their meanings differ by processor and may be unavailable in a VM. Gregg's examples demonstrate how to collect evidence and note tracing overhead. [[perf]]\n\n Derive the upper bound\nIf fraction p of baseline runtime improves by factor s and the rest is unchanged, normalized new time is (1-p) + p/s. Overall speedup is its reciprocal. With p = 0.6 and s = 2, new time is 0.7 and speedup about 1.43. Even an infinitely fast improved component cannot exceed 1/(1-p) under these assumptions.\n\nThe assumptions matter: workload is fixed, overhead is accounted for, and the improvement does not change unaffected costs. Real parallelization can introduce synchronization, memory traffic, and a different bottleneck.\n\n Relate work to data movement\nArithmetic intensity is useful operations per byte transferred at a specified memory boundary. If a toy machine sustains 40 GB/s and an algorithm performs two useful operations per eight bytes transferred, its bandwidth bound is 10 billion operations/s at that boundary. Compare with the compute bound and take the lower bound on achievable throughput in this simplified model.\n\nDo not substitute source-level bytes for measured memory traffic without checking reuse and write policies. Different cache levels have different boundaries.\n\n::: deep Noise is an investigation input\nWarm caches, page faults, CPU frequency, thermal limits, background work, allocator state, and input distributions can change results. Interleave A/B runs or otherwise control drift. Compare distributions, not just one minimum. Establish a stable baseline before interpreting a small difference.\n\nGregg's USE method prompts resource questions about utilization, saturation, and errors. Apply that as a hypothesis checklist when a benchmark becomes a system investigation. Compiler optimizations can also remove or transform supposedly equal work, so inspect the generated program. [[use]] [[opt]]\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nA fivefold improvement affects only 20% of baseline runtime. Calculate the overall speedup. Then name one observation that would refute your claim that the improved region is responsible for a large total gain.\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nNew time is 0.8 + 0.2/5 = 0.84; speedup is about 1.19. A twofold overall gain cannot follow from those fixed fractions alone. Either your fractions, measurement, workload, or “unaffected” assumption is wrong. A profile showing the region was only a small share of the original run would challenge the attribution.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nBefore Lab 03, fill in: workload, correctness oracle, independent variable, predicted mechanism, confounders, raw measurements, and a result that would change your mind. This becomes the capstone's measurement protocol.\n"}, {"id": 18, "unit": 5, "title": "Map addresses to cache sets", "path": "lessons/0018-map-addresses-to-cache-sets.html", "win": "Derive hits and misses from an explicit cache model.", "minutes": 25, "search": "Map addresses to cache sets Derive hits and misses from an explicit cache model.  A cache stores blocks\nDefine a teaching cache with S sets, E lines per set, and B bytes per line. Data capacity is SEB, excluding tags and metadata. A byte address selects a block number floor(address/B), a set block mod S, and a tag floor(block/S). The byte offset is address mod B.\n\nFor powers of two, these calculations correspond to splitting address bits. With B = 16 and S = 8, four low bits are offset, the next three select the set, and the remaining bits form the tag. Do not confuse byte address with block number.\n\n Work one trace exactly\nUse a direct-mapped cache (E = 1) with four sets and eight-byte lines. Begin empty. Access byte addresses 0, 1, 32, 0, 8:\n\n| Address | Block | Set | Tag | Result |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | miss; fill |\n| 1 | 0 | 0 | 0 | hit |\n| 32 | 4 | 0 | 1 | miss; evict block 0 |\n| 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | miss; evict block 4 |\n| 8 | 1 | 1 | 0 | miss; fill |\n\nOne hit in five accesses gives a 20% hit rate. The cache has unused capacity elsewhere, yet addresses 0 and 32 repeatedly displace each other. That is a conflict, not proof that total data capacity was exhausted.\n\n Associativity changes the constraint\nWith two lines per set, both blocks can coexist in set zero. Replacement policy becomes relevant once a third competing block arrives. The workbench uses least recently used replacement, starts empty, and counts individual read addresses. It does not model stores, prefetching, multiple cache levels, coherence, or exact processor timing.\n\nThese omissions are deliberate. A deterministic model teaches mapping; it does not predict your laptop's runtime from an arbitrary trace. Real processor cache indexing can include additional mechanisms, and some levels use virtual/physical address combinations. Consult target documentation before transferring the simple bit split literally. [[intel]]\n\n::: deep Miss rate is only one term\nA simple average-memory-access-time model is hit_time + miss_ratemiss_penalty. If hit time is one model unit, miss rate 0.1, and additional miss penalty 40, average time is five units. The penalty is additional time, so do not count the initial lookup twice.\n\nReal execution can overlap misses, prefetch data, or stall on dependencies. Two traces with the same miss rate can have different runtime because their accesses expose different parallelism. Drepper's memory investigation provides a practitioner's account of the hierarchy; its historical timings are not current hardware constants. [[memory]]\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nUse four sets, eight-byte lines, one way, and an empty cache. Predict 0, 8, 0, 32, 0 without running the simulator. Then change only associativity to two.\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nDirect-mapped outcomes are miss, miss, hit, miss, miss: one hit. Two-way outcomes are miss, miss, hit, miss, hit: two hits. Addresses 0 and 32 share a set; address 8 uses another. If your answer changes the set calculation when adding ways, revisit which parameter controls set count.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nGenerate two equal-length traces with the same number of unique blocks but different miss rates. Explain why “working set fits in total capacity” is necessary in some cases but insufficient for this cache mapping.\n"}, {"id": 19, "unit": 5, "title": "Locality and blocking", "path": "lessons/0019-locality-and-blocking.html", "win": "Predict which traversal reuses a line before it is evicted.", "minutes": 25, "search": "Locality and blocking Predict which traversal reuses a line before it is evicted.  Spatial and temporal reuse are distinct\nSpatial locality means nearby bytes are used together. Temporal locality means recently used data is used again soon. A sequential pass can have strong spatial locality while touching each element only once. A tiny hot lookup table can have strong temporal locality even when indices look irregular.\n\nA cache line pays for a group of nearby bytes. If an eight-byte element sits in a 64-byte line, a contiguous traversal can use eight elements from that line. A stride of one large row between loads may use only one element before moving elsewhere. This is a concrete reason for different traffic, not a slogan that “arrays are fast.”\n\n Reuse distance connects code to capacity\nImagine iterating columns outside and rows inside on a 2048-by-2048 matrix of four-byte elements. Consecutive inner-loop accesses are 8192 bytes apart. Returning to a nearby element in the same row may happen only after touching thousands of other lines. Whether that earlier line survives depends on the cache's sets, capacity, associativity, and other traffic.\n\nReversing loop order makes neighboring inner-loop accesses contiguous. It may improve useful bytes per fetched line even if both versions perform the same arithmetic. Compilers may transform loops, so inspect generated behavior before attributing every difference to the source order. [[opt]]\n\n Blocking bounds the live footprint\nFor a matrix operation that reuses tiles from three arrays, a first footprint estimate for square tile side T and element size d is 3TTd. With T = 32 and d = 8, that is 24,576 bytes. This suggests a candidate tile size, not a guarantee of fit: associativity conflicts, other data, write policies, and alignment matter.\n\nChoose several nearby tile sizes and test correctness and performance. Include sizes not divisible by the tile side. Edge tiles require clipped bounds; optimizing only square multiples is not a complete implementation.\n\n A useful laboratory result is allowed to be boring\nLab 03 checks that two traversal orders produce the same integer checksum, then records repeated timings. If your matrix fits in cache or the compiler interchanges loops, the difference may be small. Report that outcome honestly. Increase the working set within your machine's limits or inspect the compiler's loop transformations before inventing a cache story. [[perf]]\n\n::: deep Data layout is another loop transformation\nAn array of records loads unused fields if the loop needs only one member. Separate arrays can make the needed field contiguous, improving line utilization and vectorization. But code that uses all fields together may favor records, and reorganizing data has allocation and interface costs.\n\nDrepper's investigation motivates thinking about traffic and locality rather than instruction count alone. Treat its examples as mechanisms to investigate on your hardware, not as universal rankings. A complete argument names the hot fields, access order, element size, cache boundary, and measured consequence. [[memory]]\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nA row contains 1024 eight-byte elements. What is the byte stride when traversing a fixed column down successive rows? How many elements fit in an assumed 64-byte line during a contiguous row traversal?\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nThe stride is 10248 = 8192 bytes. Eight elements fit in a 64-byte line, subject to alignment at the beginning/end. The line size is an explicit assumption; inspect your target if it matters. Knowing those numbers does not yet prove the miss rate, because retention and mapping still matter.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nExtend Lab 03 with blocked traversal and a non-multiple matrix dimension. Keep the exact checksum oracle. Before running it, predict which tile sizes are too small to reduce overhead and which may exceed a chosen cache footprint.\n"}, {"id": 20, "unit": 5, "title": "Coherence and false sharing", "path": "lessons/0020-coherence-and-false-sharing.html", "win": "Distinguish correct sharing from unnecessary cache-line contention.", "minutes": 25, "search": "Coherence and false sharing Distinguish correct sharing from unnecessary cache-line contention.  The hardware tracks lines, not intentions\nTwo cores can cache copies of the same memory region. A coherence mechanism coordinates access so the architecture's rules for a location are respected. A core that writes may need exclusive ownership of a line, invalidating or changing another core's copy. This traffic can become expensive when ownership moves repeatedly.\n\nIf each thread updates a different counter but both counters occupy one coherence line, their logical independence does not prevent hardware contention. This is false sharing. If they update the same counter, the sharing is real; padding cannot remove the logical coordination requirement.\n\n First establish a valid program\nDo not benchmark plain unsynchronized increments to shared C objects and describe any odd output as “the cache did not flush.” A C data race is a language-level correctness failure. Use synchronization or distinct independently accessed objects with valid lifetime and publication. Later lessons establish those contracts.\n\nFor a simple false-sharing experiment, use one relaxed atomic counter per thread, then compare tightly adjacent counters with deliberately separated ones. Check alignment and the target's relevant line size. Keep the total work and memory ordering constant between variants. Atomic counters make the increments well-defined; they do not make the contention disappear.\n\n Design an alternative explanation\nSuppose separation improves elapsed time. Competing explanations include changed alignment, different compiler code, scheduling, migration, and a larger working set. Inspect addresses and instructions. Repeated runs and appropriate hardware events can strengthen the coherence explanation, but event names and availability vary.\n\nA stronger design often accumulates into thread-local values and combines once. This reduces the frequency of shared writes instead of merely separating them. The reduction itself needs a sound ownership/synchronization protocol. Measure total runtime, including the final combination.\n\n Coherence is not a whole-program order\nCoherence concerns coordination of accesses to a location or line under an architecture. It does not by itself promise that independent writes become visible in the source order needed for a multi-object publication protocol. Compiler reordering and CPU memory ordering add separate constraints. User-space C atomics express those constraints at the language level; hardware manuals describe how targets implement them. [[intel]]\n\n::: deep Keep memory models in their own domain\nThe Linux kernel memory-barrier guide provides detailed examples of ordering and compiler effects. It explicitly describes kernel mechanisms and caveats; copying a kernel barrier into a C user program is not a substitute for the C memory model. [[barriers]]\n\nDrepper's paper offers useful historical coherence experiments and layout reasoning. Modern cores, interconnects, and NUMA systems vary. In NUMA, placement adds another dimension: local and remote physical memory can have different costs, even when cache layout and synchronization are correct. Pinning and placement can help experiments but also change the workload you are measuring. [[memory]]\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nWhy might one atomic increment per item scale worse than one atomic addition per completed batch? Does relaxing memory order remove the requirement to coordinate writes to the same counter?\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nBatching reduces the number of shared ownership and atomic-update operations. Relaxed ordering can remove some ordering constraints, but each update to the same atomic object still participates in its atomic modification order and must coordinate correctly. It is not an instruction to allow lost updates. If a batch's result must be visible with other data, specify the publication protocol too.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nAfter Lab 08's correctness work, add the optional counter-layout benchmark. Report correctness separately from throughput, and explain whether your intervention reduced true sharing, false sharing, or both. Do not infer a race merely from poor scaling.\n"}, {"id": 21, "unit": 6, "title": "Translate a virtual address", "path": "lessons/0021-translate-a-virtual-address.html", "win": "Split an address into a page number and offset, then translate it.", "minutes": 25, "search": "Translate a virtual address Split an address into a page number and offset, then translate it.  Addresses are interpreted in a context\nA pointer's numerical address in a user process is normally a virtual address. Translation maps a virtual page to a physical frame under that process's address-space context and checks permitted access. Two processes can use the same virtual number for unrelated physical storage; they can also deliberately map the same physical storage at different virtual numbers.\n\nAssume a toy 16-bit virtual address and 256-byte pages. The low eight bits are the offset; the high eight bits are the virtual page number, or VPN. Virtual address 0x1234 has VPN 0x12 and offset 0x34. If VPN 0x12 maps to physical frame 0x2A, the physical address is 0x2A34. This is our original example of the paging mechanism described in OSTEP. [[paging]]\n\n Translation and permission are separate outcomes\nA page-table entry can encode a frame and flags such as presence and allowed access, depending on architecture. A read-only mapping cannot satisfy a user write merely because a frame exists. An absent translation may lead to an operating-system fault handler; it is not automatically a disk read or a fatal crash.\n\nThe browser translator uses a small explicit mapping table with present and readable flags. It is a teaching model. Real x86-64 and AArch64 page tables have architecture-specific formats, multiple levels, and more permission/context details. Do not treat the toy table as a register layout.\n\n Map regions, not individual variables\nOperating systems manage mappings at page granularity. A small out-of-bounds C access can remain inside a valid writable page, so it may not trigger a hardware protection fault. Conversely, a valid request for an as-yet-unbacked mapped page can fault and recover normally. This is why page protection and C object safety solve different problems.\n\nOn Linux, inspect /proc/PID/maps for a known process. It shows virtual ranges and permissions, not the address of each C object or a complete residency report. Dynamic allocation may obtain memory through different underlying mechanisms; “the heap” is a useful label, not a guarantee that every allocation comes from one contiguous range. [[maps]]\n\n::: deep Shared mappings and aliases\nA file can be mapped into multiple processes. A shared mapping and a private copy-on-write mapping differ in what writes mean. mmap specifies mapping flags and error behavior; callers must test for MAP_FAILED, which is not the same failure sentinel as malloc's NULL. [[mmap]]\n\nAn address-to-frame diagram should label the address space, page size, VPN, frame, offset, and permissions. Without the address-space label, a shared numerical address may be mistaken for shared storage. Without the permission check, translation may look like unconditional arithmetic.\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nWith 1024-byte pages, split virtual address 0x2ABC. If its VPN maps to frame 7, calculate the physical address. Then say what additional condition must hold for a user write to succeed.\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nTen low bits form the offset: 0x2ABC = 101024 + 700, so VPN is 10 and offset is 700 (0x2BC). Physical address is 71024 + 700 = 7868, or 0x1EBC. The mapping and access mode must permit the write, and other architecture-specific checks must succeed. A correct sum does not bypass protection.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nDraw two processes where virtual address 0x4000 maps to different frames. Then draw a deliberate shared mapping at unequal virtual addresses. Explain why comparing printed pointers across processes cannot establish sharing.\n"}, {"id": 22, "unit": 6, "title": "Page tables and TLBs", "path": "lessons/0022-page-tables-and-tlbs.html", "win": "Distinguish translation-cache misses from faults and data-cache misses.", "minutes": 25, "search": "Page tables and TLBs Distinguish translation-cache misses from faults and data-cache misses.  Caching the map is different from caching the data\nA translation lookaside buffer caches address translations. A data cache stores data blocks. The same load can hit in one and miss in the other. Conflating those events leads to weak performance explanations.\n\nA TLB miss means the translation is absent from that translation cache. If page-table entries describe a valid accessible mapping, translation can be recovered, often through a hardware walk on our main targets. A page fault means the access requires operating-system handling because a translation or permission condition was not satisfied. A data-cache miss means the requested data block is absent at that cache level. OSTEP's TLB chapter develops this distinction. [[tlb]]\n\n Derive why one flat table is expensive\nAssume 48-bit virtual addresses, 4 KiB pages, and eight-byte leaf entries. There are 2^(48-12) = 2^36 virtual pages. A fully populated flat table would need 2^368 = 2^39 bytes, or 512 GiB, for one address space. This is an illustrative address-space calculation, not a claim that every x86 processor uses exactly 48 virtual bits today.\n\nMulti-level page tables allocate lower-level tables only for populated ranges. With 4 KiB table pages and eight-byte entries, each table holds 512 entries, consuming nine index bits per level. Four such levels plus a 12-bit offset account for 48 bits in a simplified common x86-64 layout. Other configurations use different depths and page sizes. [[intel]]\n\n Sparse mappings save memory, walks add work\nIf a program touches only a few ranges, most lower-level tables need not exist. But a miss in translation caches can require several dependent memory references to walk the structure. Caches of page-table data and intermediate translations reduce this cost. A simple “every load always requires four extra DRAM accesses” model is therefore too pessimistic.\n\nTLB reach is roughly the number of entries times the page size for a particular translation-cache structure. A 64-entry toy TLB with 4 KiB pages covers 256 KiB if each entry maps a distinct ordinary page. Larger pages can increase reach, but have allocation, fragmentation, permission-granularity, and OS-policy tradeoffs.\n\n::: deep Why strides can stress two hierarchies\nA loop reading one byte from each of thousands of pages uses little useful data per page and may exceed TLB reach. It can also create data-cache misses. To distinguish these effects experimentally, vary page count, within-page reuse, and data footprint, then choose appropriate events if available.\n\nContext switches require translations to remain associated with the correct address space. Address-space tags can preserve useful entries across some switches; changing page tables may require invalidation or coordination across cores. Consult the target and OS rather than claiming that every context switch flushes every TLB entry. [[paging]]\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nCompute the entry count and byte size of a flat table for 32-bit virtual addresses, 4 KiB pages, and four-byte entries. Does a TLB miss prove the corresponding page is absent from RAM?\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nThere are 2^20 pages and entries, requiring 2^22 bytes, or 4 MiB. No: the mapping and data may be resident while only the cached translation is missing. If you answered one million bytes, you counted entries but forgot the entry size.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nDesign two access patterns with the same number of useful bytes but different page footprints. State what observation would support a translation-pressure hypothesis and what confounder you would need to control.\n"}, {"id": 23, "unit": 6, "title": "Faults, copy-on-write, and mmap", "path": "lessons/0023-faults-copy-on-write-and-mmap.html", "win": "Explain a recoverable page fault and distinguish mapping from residency.", "minutes": 25, "search": "Faults, copy-on-write, and mmap Explain a recoverable page fault and distinguish mapping from residency.  A fault can be useful work\nA user load or store can trigger a synchronous exception when translation or permission checks need help. The operating system examines the address, mapping, and access. It may establish backing storage and resume the instruction, or reject the access and report a fault to the process.\n\nDemand allocation lets a process reserve a virtual range without immediately populating every page with private physical storage. Consequently a successful allocation call does not prove every byte is resident or that future physical-memory pressure cannot cause problems. Observe the distinction between virtual size, resident memory, and application-requested bytes.\n\n Copy-on-write preserves separate semantics\nAfter fork, parent and child have separate address spaces with initially corresponding contents. An implementation can share physical pages read-only until a write requires a private version. The performance trick must preserve the process contract: one process's ordinary private write is not a write to the other's independent object. [[fork]]\n\nThis explains why a tiny child that immediately executes another program need not copy every byte of a large parent's memory eagerly. It also explains why a child that dirties many pages can incur substantial memory work. “Fork is free” and “fork always copies the entire heap immediately” are both inadequate models.\n\n Map a file deliberately\nFor file-backed mmap, choose shared versus private behavior, access permissions, length, and offset. Check for MAP_FAILED. Offsets have page-alignment constraints; map a suitably aligned range and account for an internal displacement when the logical record starts later. Keep the file's length and lifetime assumptions explicit. [[mmap]]\n\nA mapped load can fault when the page is first accessed. Access beyond valid backing-file conditions can fail in ways different from a checked read returning an error code. Another process truncating a mapped file changes the experiment's assumptions. The apparent convenience of array indexing does not remove I/O failure modes.\n\n Inspect without overclaiming\nOn Linux, compare the process map before and after a mapping or allocation. Then distinguish that map from residency/accounting observations. /proc/PID/maps describes ranges and permissions; it cannot alone answer how much private physical memory the process currently consumes. [[maps]]\n\n::: deep Minor, major, and fatal are different axes\nLinux fault accounting distinguishes faults that require I/O from those that do not under its documented accounting behavior. A recoverable copy-on-write or demand-zero event can be a minor fault. A major fault generally involves I/O work. An invalid access can instead lead to a signal such as SIGSEGV; a backing-file problem can produce SIGBUS on relevant mappings.\n\nAvoid diagnosing every segmentation fault as “out of RAM” or every major fault as “swap.” File-backed demand paging is another source of I/O. Connect the fault to the mapping and workload before choosing a remedy.\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nA process maps a 1 GiB file privately, reads one byte, then writes one byte. Does that imply one GiB of private resident memory? Which events might differ between the read and the write?\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nNo. Mapping establishes a range; access determines which pages need backing and become resident. The read may establish or reuse a file-backed page. A private write may require copy-on-write storage for that page. Actual accounting depends on OS state, page size, readahead, and existing cache. Estimate at page granularity rather than equating mapped length with private RSS.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nIn Lab 04, separate the allocator's requested bytes from its arena capacity and the OS's mapped pages. For an extension, write a small private-mapping experiment and predict behavior before observing memory accounting.\n"}, {"id": 24, "unit": 7, "title": "Allocation and ownership", "path": "lessons/0024-allocation-and-ownership.html", "win": "Resize an allocation without losing the only pointer on failure.", "minutes": 25, "search": "Allocation and ownership Resize an allocation without losing the only pointer on failure.  An allocation has a protocol\nA successful allocation creates storage a caller can own. Borrowers may use it under bounds and lifetime constraints; an owner eventually releases it exactly once. C does not enforce this protocol automatically, so make it visible in API documentation and error paths.\n\nFor an array allocation, validate the element count and multiplication before calling the allocator. Check success before dereferencing. Avoid calling an allocation of zero bytes a portable writable object: establish an explicit empty-state policy. The allocator interface documents the behavior and important edge cases. [[malloc]]\n\n Resizing changes the object's identity\nFor a nonzero new byte size:\n\nc\nvoid candidate = realloc(buffer, new_bytes);\nif (candidate == NULL) {\n    / buffer is still owned and must be handled /\n} else {\n    buffer = candidate;\n    / previous pointers into the old allocation are invalid /\n}\n\n\nCheck new_bytes arithmetic first. On success, realloc may move storage, preserving the appropriate prefix. Even an unchanged numerical address does not justify continuing to use old interior pointer values; recompute offsets from the new base under the new object's bounds. New bytes beyond the preserved prefix are not automatically initialized.\n\nThe direct assignment buffer = realloc(buffer, new_bytes) loses the only pointer on failure if there is no other owner. That is a real ownership leak, even though no invalid memory access occurs immediately.\n\n Error paths are control-flow ownership\nWrite a resource table for a function: allocation A, descriptor B, allocation C. After each acquisition, ask which earlier resources remain owned if the next operation fails. A single cleanup section can be clearer than duplicated release logic, provided each resource has a well-defined empty state and ownership transfer is explicit.\n\nDo not free borrowed memory merely because a function no longer needs it. Conversely, returning an owning pointer without documenting the caller's obligation makes leaks likely. Ownership is a part of the interface as much as parameter types.\n\n::: deep Growth policy is an algorithmic choice\nGrowing capacity by one element on every insertion can require quadratic total copying when resizing moves allocations. Geometric growth can give amortized constant insertion cost, but increases slack and still requires overflow checks when computing the next capacity.\n\nDistinguish logical length, allocated capacity, and byte size. For a vector of records, valid initialized elements lie below length; available storage lies below capacity; bytes equal capacity times element size only after checked multiplication. An allocation fitting its capacity does not imply every element has been initialized. ASan can catch some invalid accesses, but it does not generally prove your logical initialization invariant. [[asan]]\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nA vector has length 9 and capacity 16. After a successful resize to capacity 32, which facts remain true automatically, and which must the program establish? Consider pointers into the buffer, existing elements, and new storage.\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nThe preserved prefix retains its contents up to the lesser old/new size. Old interior pointers must be replaced with pointers based on the new allocation. Length does not become 32 automatically; the new capacity does not initialize new logical elements. The program must update bookkeeping consistently and preserve a valid ownership path. The language allocation rules are in §7.22.3. [[c]]\n:::\n\n Transfer\nWrite a small vector API contract without implementing it. Specify ownership on success and failure for creation, append, resize, and destruction. Then audit Lab 04's arena reset: which borrowed pointers does one reset invalidate?\n"}, {"id": 25, "unit": 7, "title": "Design a small allocator", "path": "lessons/0025-design-a-small-allocator.html", "win": "Derive alignment and splitting rules and test allocator invariants.", "minutes": 30, "search": "Design a small allocator Derive alignment and splitting rules and test allocator invariants.  An arena exposes the core obligations\nAn arena reserves backing storage and advances a cursor for allocations. It can release everything at once by resetting the cursor. It is useful when many objects share a lifetime, such as one parsed request. It cannot individually free arbitrary objects unless you add more machinery.\n\nBefore returning an address, align the cursor and check remaining capacity. For power-of-two alignment A, padding is (A - used%A)%A. Check padding <= capacity-used and then size <= capacity-used-padding before advancing. These subtraction-based checks avoid overflowing a speculative used+padding+size.\n\nThe arena's base must itself satisfy the required alignment. Aligning an offset cannot repair a misaligned base. Lab 04 uses a max_align_t-aligned buffer and supports ordinary alignments up to that limit; it is not an over-aligned general-purpose replacement for malloc.\n\n Free lists buy flexibility with metadata\nA reusable allocator can track blocks with sizes, allocation state, and links between free blocks. To serve a request, round for alignment, add overhead safely, choose a free region, and split only if the remainder can represent a valid free block. On release, coalesce adjacent free blocks where the representation permits.\n\nA first-fit policy chooses the first sufficiently large block. Best-fit seeks a close size match but may leave tiny unusable fragments and add search cost. Segregated lists group sizes for faster selection. Policy is a workload tradeoff, not a universal ranking. OSTEP develops these design choices and fragmentation distinctions. [[free]]\n\n State invariants before writing code\nFor an arena: cursor never exceeds capacity, returned ranges do not overlap while live, each return meets alignment, and failure leaves the arena unchanged. After reset, previous arena objects must no longer be used.\n\nFor a free-list allocator: every block's size is valid and aligned, blocks partition the managed region, allocated blocks do not overlap, free-list links refer only to free blocks, and each free block appears exactly once. If immediate coalescing is promised, no adjacent free blocks remain separate. These invariants guide tests more effectively than “allocate ten things and see.”\n\n Measure fragmentation honestly\nInternal fragmentation is unused space inside an allocated block, such as rounding slack. External fragmentation is free space divided into regions that cannot satisfy a request, despite sufficient total free bytes. Metadata overhead is another cost. Keep them separate when comparing allocators.\n\n::: deep Space and throughput can conflict\nAn allocator can reduce search time by reserving more classes or keeping per-thread caches, increasing memory retained outside the application's current live set. Releasing an object does not guarantee an immediate drop in process RSS. The system allocator may keep storage for reuse rather than return whole pages to the OS. [[malloc]]\n\nA reset-based arena avoids many free-list issues but moves responsibility into lifetime grouping. If one long-lived object keeps a request arena alive, the entire arena can remain retained. The right interface is the one whose lifetime matches the workload, not the one with the shortest allocator implementation.\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nAn arena base is eight-byte aligned, cursor 13, capacity 40, and request size 16 with alignment eight. Calculate padding, returned offset, and new cursor. Would a following 16-byte request at the same alignment fit?\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nPadding is three; return offset is 16 and new cursor 32. A following aligned 16-byte request would end at 48, exceeding capacity, so it must fail without changing the cursor. The failure check should compare against remaining space before computing an overflowing end offset in a real large-size case. Alignment contracts follow the language's object requirements. [[c]]\n:::\n\n Transfer\nComplete Lab 04's arena investigation. Then sketch a free-list extension, including the smallest splittable remainder and a coalescing test. Do not call the extension complete without the structural invariants.\n"}, {"id": 26, "unit": 7, "title": "Memory forensics", "path": "lessons/0026-memory-forensics.html", "win": "Choose evidence that distinguishes leaks, retention, corruption, and virtual reservations.", "minutes": 25, "search": "Memory forensics Choose evidence that distinguishes leaks, retention, corruption, and virtual reservations.  Similar graphs can have different causes\nA process's memory usage rises. Possible explanations include more live application data, lost owning pointers, allocator retention, file-backed residency, copy-on-write, thread stacks, and reserved virtual ranges. “Memory leak” is one hypothesis, not a synonym for every upward line.\n\nStart by specifying the metric: virtual mapped bytes, resident pages, private dirty pages, or requested live allocation bytes. These measure different layers. A Linux memory map shows ranges and permissions; it is not a heap ownership graph. [[maps]]\n\n Four investigations, four kinds of evidence\nFor a suspected leak, track allocations and ownership over a repeated steady-state workload. After equivalent work, does unreachable or unreleased allocation state grow? Leak tooling can help where available; platform support differs.\n\nFor corruption, find the earliest invalid access with ASan or a watchpoint and a reproducing input. The later allocator abort is a witness. For excessive retention, compare live requested bytes with allocator-held capacity and RSS across repeated cycles. For a huge virtual reservation, observe how many pages are actually accessed/resident rather than assuming the entire mapping is physical memory.\n\nASan is primarily an access/lifetime instrumentation tool; leak detection may be supplied by related runtime components and is not equally available on every platform. It does not replace a domain-specific ownership argument. [[asan]]\n\n Build a repeatable memory experiment\nChoose a workload cycle, such as parse 1000 requests and discard all per-request state. Establish what should survive the cycle: configuration, caches, worker threads. Warm up intentionally, then measure several equivalent cycles. A bounded cache can legitimately grow during warmup and plateau.\n\nRecord input sizes, object counts, requested bytes, and process metrics. If requested live bytes return to baseline but RSS does not, investigate retention and fragmentation before declaring a leak. If live bytes rise by one request object per cycle, inspect the release path or a reference that unnecessarily extends lifetime.\n\n Repair the violated contract\nA leak repair restores ownership on every relevant path. A corruption repair establishes bounds or lifetime. A retention repair may require different pooling or object lifetimes rather than more calls to free. A capacity problem may require a bound or backpressure. These remedies address different mechanisms.\n\n::: deep A stable average can still hide a failure\nA mean request size can look harmless while rare large requests retain oversized buffers per worker. With 100 workers each keeping one 16 MiB buffer, retained capacity reaches roughly 1.6 GiB before other state. An experiment must reflect tail sizes, concurrency, and reuse policy.\n\nThe allocator's documented behavior explains why release and OS reclamation do not have a one-to-one relationship. Gregg's resource-oriented method helps frame memory saturation and error questions without beginning from one favorite metric. [[malloc]] [[use]]\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nAfter each workload cycle, requested live bytes return to 2 MiB, mapped virtual bytes remain at 1 GiB, and RSS stabilizes at 50 MiB. What can you conclude, and what cannot you conclude?\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nThe observed requested-live metric and RSS plateau do not show unbounded growth in those measurements. They are compatible with reservation and retention. They do not prove every object is correctly owned, that all paths are safe, or that a different input distribution cannot cause growth. You need definitions and coverage for the metrics before calling the program leak-free.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nWrite a one-page memory investigation for Lab 04 or the capstone: workload cycle, expected survivors, metric definitions, two competing hypotheses, discriminating observations, and the limitation of your conclusion.\n"}, {"id": 27, "unit": 8, "title": "Exceptions, interrupts, and syscalls", "path": "lessons/0027-exceptions-interrupts-and-syscalls.html", "win": "Explain how control crosses the user/kernel boundary and returns.", "minutes": 25, "search": "Exceptions, interrupts, and syscalls Explain how control crosses the user/kernel boundary and returns.  Control flow is larger than function calls\nA normal call transfers between instructions in a process under an ABI. A system call deliberately requests an operating-system service through a privileged entry mechanism. A fault or other synchronous exception is associated with the current instruction. An external interrupt arrives independently of that user's current source line.\n\nThe precise categories and names vary by architecture. On x86, consult the architecture manuals for interrupt, fault, and trap semantics instead of mapping every event onto a C function call. The OS saves and restores required execution state, chooses a handler, and decides whether and where execution continues. [[intel]]\n\n User code requests a service\nCalling the C library's read interface may eventually enter the kernel to obtain bytes from a descriptor. The library function's calling convention and the kernel's system-call ABI are separate interfaces. A compiler-generated ordinary function call is not automatically a privilege transition.\n\nThe kernel validates arguments and permissions. For a pointer to a user buffer, a plausible numerical address is not enough. The requested range must be usable under the operation's rules. A successful system call may still transfer fewer bytes than requested. A failed call is reported through its interface's return/error contract. [[read]]\n\n A process can stop being the running process\nDuring kernel work, a scheduler may run something else, especially if the caller waits for I/O. A context switch saves enough state to resume one execution context and restores another. This is distinct from a mode transition: the kernel can handle a short service and return to the same process without scheduling a different one.\n\nLikewise, a library call may be satisfied mostly in user space and perform no system call on that invocation. Do not infer privilege transitions merely from source-level function names.\n\n Signals are an OS-to-process interface\nA signal is not the CPU exception handler itself. The OS may deliver a signal in response to a fault, a terminal action, another process, or a timer. Delivery can run a user-space handler under constraints or take a default action. Blocking, pending state, and disposition are separate parts of the model. [[signal]]\n\n::: deep Restarting an instruction is a contract\nA recoverable page fault can establish a mapping and resume the faulting instruction. Other synchronous events may resume after an instruction or terminate execution. Precise exceptions require the visible architectural state to correspond to a meaningful point even when the microarchitecture did speculative work.\n\nFor debugging, ask: which operation caused the transition, what state was saved, which handler/interface runs, and what continuation is permitted? That question set connects a syscall trace, a debugger backtrace, and a fault report without pretending they are the same view.\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nA program waits in read, another process runs, a device completes work, and the original program later resumes. Identify a syscall boundary, a scheduling event, and a possible interrupt without collapsing them into one event.\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nThe user enters the kernel to request read. If bytes are unavailable, the task may block and the scheduler can run another task. Device completion may generate an interrupt that lets the kernel make progress and wake the waiter. The waiter runs when scheduled, and the syscall eventually returns under its byte-count/error contract. Exact paths depend on the descriptor and implementation.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nOn a Linux learning environment, trace Lab 05 with strace -f if available. Label library-visible operations separately from kernel-visible calls. Explain which conclusions the trace can support and which instruction-level details it cannot show.\n"}, {"id": 28, "unit": 8, "title": "Fork, exec, and wait", "path": "lessons/0028-fork-exec-and-wait.html", "win": "Account for parent and child paths, image replacement, and reaping.", "minutes": 30, "search": "Fork, exec, and wait Account for parent and child paths, image replacement, and reaping.  One call, two continuations\nAfter successful fork, the parent receives the child's positive PID and the child receives zero. A negative return indicates failure in the parent; no child was created. Both successful paths continue after the call. Their ordinary private variables begin with corresponding values but subsequently belong to separate address spaces. [[fork]]\n\nDraw the control-flow tree before predicting output. After two unconditional successful forks there can be four processes, because both existing processes execute the second call. Conditional branches can change the tree; a memorized power-of-two rule is not a substitute for tracing them.\n\n Replace an image deliberately\nA child often rearranges descriptors and calls an exec-family function. Success replaces its program image and does not return. Failure returns to the old code and requires an error path. Arguments are an array of pointers terminated by a null pointer; the strings have their own terminators. Process arguments and shell command parsing are not the same thing. [[exec]]\n\nAfter a failed exec in a forked child, _exit is usually the appropriate termination primitive for the simple pattern in this course. Calling normal exit can run inherited buffered-output flushing or cleanup in an unintended context. Before fork, buffered stdio data is user-space state that can be copied into both processes.\n\n Reap and interpret status\nA terminated child leaves status for its parent. waitpid collects it; retry appropriately if interrupted. The returned status is encoded. Check WIFEXITED before WEXITSTATUS; check WIFSIGNALED before WTERMSIG. Do not treat the raw integer as the child's return code. [[wait]]\n\nA zombie is a terminated child whose status has not been reaped, not a still-running worker consuming ordinary execution time. A parent needs a deliberate policy for every child: wait now, reap later, or hand responsibility to a well-defined supervisor.\n\n Inheritance has multiple layers\nFile descriptors survive fork and refer to shared open-file descriptions, so file offsets can be shared even while user-space memory is private. Descriptor close-on-exec flags affect what survives a successful exec. The parent and child each need to close their own unused pipe endpoints. A pipe reader cannot observe EOF while any writer endpoint remains open.\n\n::: deep Forking a multithreaded process is harder\nAfter fork in a multithreaded process, only the calling thread remains in the child, while copied library state may reflect locks held by vanished threads. Between fork and exec, POSIX imposes strict constraints on operations the child can safely perform. The lab uses a simple single-threaded parent and a controlled child path; do not generalize arbitrary C library activity there to a threaded server.\n\nCreation, replacement, and reaping solve separate problems. A compact shell pipeline can involve all three plus descriptor ownership. Use a process/descriptor diagram to keep the obligations visible.\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nA parent forks, the child successfully execs a utility, and the parent waits. How many processes were created by fork and by exec? Why might output without a newline printed before fork appear twice when redirected?\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nFork creates one child; exec creates no additional process. Redirected stdio may leave the pre-fork text buffered in user memory. Both processes inherit that buffer, and normal exit can flush both copies. Flush deliberately before fork or avoid that output pattern; use the child termination protocol appropriate to the design.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nLab 05 builds a small pipeline. Produce a table listing every descriptor in parent and child before and after each close/dup operation. Then remove one close in a scratch copy and predict whether the result is a leak, a hang waiting for EOF, or an output-routing error.\n"}, {"id": 29, "unit": 8, "title": "Signals without races", "path": "lessons/0029-signals-without-races.html", "win": "Design a minimal handler and explain the lost-wakeup race.", "minutes": 30, "search": "Signals without races Design a minimal handler and explain the lost-wakeup race.  Delivery is not an ordinary callback\nAn asynchronous signal can interrupt code between operations that a library assumes are coordinated. A handler that calls printf or malloc can reenter internal state that was already mid-update. The list of async-signal-safe functions is a real contract, not a style preference. [[safe]]\n\nFor a simple single-threaded application, let the handler assign a volatile sig_atomic_t flag. Let ordinary control flow do the complex work. This limited use of volatile is about communication with an asynchronous handler; it is not a general inter-thread synchronization mechanism.\n\n Masks close a timing window\nSuppose main code checks child_done == 0, then calls pause. The child terminates and the handler sets the flag after the check but before pause. The signal is already handled, so the subsequent pause can sleep indefinitely waiting for another event.\n\nThe repair must coordinate the predicate check with unblocking-and-waiting. A typical pattern blocks the relevant signal, checks state, then uses sigsuspend with an appropriate temporary mask so the transition to waiting is atomic relative to delivery. Upon return, recheck the predicate. Merely moving lines closer together does not remove the race. [[signal]]\n\n One notification is not one event\nStandard signals can coalesce while pending. One SIGCHLD does not necessarily correspond to one child to reap. A reaping loop calls waitpid with WNOHANG until no more exited children are currently available, handling error conditions. The program's state tracks the work; the signal is a notification to inspect that state. [[wait]]\n\nFor a more complex design, a self-pipe can translate a handler notification into a descriptor event. Its write endpoint must be nonblocking and the handler must tolerate a full pipe rather than block. Another option on Linux is a descriptor-based signal interface with a carefully managed mask. Each design still needs an explicit ownership and event-loss policy.\n\n Job control adds process groups\nA shell groups related processes into a job and coordinates which process group owns the terminal. Ctrl-C is not conceptually a loop that individually calls your handler in every process; terminal-generated signals target the foreground process group under the OS terminal rules. A full job-control shell must coordinate groups, waits, terminal state, and races across creation and exec.\n\n::: deep Handler correctness has a context\nThe simple flag pattern assumes a narrow single-threaded use. In a multithreaded program, signal masks are per-thread and process-directed delivery can select an eligible thread. Inter-thread access to ordinary state still requires the relevant language synchronization. A dedicated thread synchronously waiting for blocked signals can simplify a larger service.\n\nIf a handler touches errno, preserve and restore it when the surrounding interrupted code depends on it. Keep handler work small enough to reason about at every possible interruption point, not just when main is idle.\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nDraw the exact interleaving that loses a notification between a predicate check and pause. Then explain why a loop around that same pair still contains a window.\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nMain observes false; a signal arrives; the handler sets true and returns; main calls pause and sleeps. A loop only retries after waking, so it cannot repair sleeping after the already-consumed notification. Blocking the signal during the check and atomically changing the mask while waiting closes the relevant gap. The shared predicate remains the source of truth.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nExtend Lab 05 with controlled termination handling. Keep output and cleanup out of the handler. Write down which signals can coalesce and how the ordinary main loop notices all completed work.\n"}, {"id": 30, "unit": 9, "title": "Descriptors, offsets, and pipes", "path": "lessons/0030-descriptors-offsets-and-pipes.html", "win": "Draw descriptor ownership and explain shared offsets and pipe EOF.", "minutes": 25, "search": "Descriptors, offsets, and pipes Draw descriptor ownership and explain shared offsets and pipe EOF.  An integer is a handle, not the file\nA file descriptor is a small integer in a process's descriptor table. The entry refers to an open-file description that can hold offset and status state. Separate descriptor entries can refer to the same description; separate opens of one pathname can refer to different descriptions for the same underlying file.\n\nAfter dup, both descriptors share the open-file description's offset. Reading three bytes through one can make the next read through the other begin at byte three. They do not become independent cursors merely because their integer handles differ. The dup interface documents this sharing. [[dup]]\n\n Fork copies handles, not independent file state\nParent and child inherit descriptor entries referring to corresponding open-file descriptions. Their private C variables diverge, but shared file offsets and underlying objects can still coordinate behavior. This is an important counterexample to “fork makes everything separate.” [[fork]]\n\nClosing one descriptor removes that process's reference. It does not necessarily close every other reference to the open-file description, including references in another process. dup2 can atomically replace a target descriptor with a reference to a chosen description, making it useful for redirecting standard input or output.\n\n Pipes are lifetime protocols\nA pipe has a read end and a write end. A reader gets bytes while bytes are available; it observes EOF after the buffer is drained and all writer references are closed. If the parent accidentally retains a write endpoint, the child reader can wait forever even when the intended producer has exited.\n\nLikewise, writing when no readers remain can generate SIGPIPE or report EPIPE under the configured signal behavior. An error-handling strategy must account for the process being signaled before ordinary code sees the return value.\n\n Draw a pipeline before coding\nFor a producer connected to a child utility, label endpoints r and w. After fork, both processes initially have both endpoints. The producer closes r; the child maps r to standard input, closes the original r when appropriate, and closes w. The producer writes, closes w, and waits. Exact handling must account for descriptors already equal to the intended target in more general code.\n\nLab 05 uses a controlled invocation to make the ownership visible. Its completion check detects the resulting transformed bytes, not a particular interleaving of diagnostic output.\n\n::: deep “Everything is a file” has limits\nDescriptors provide a common handle abstraction, but regular files, pipes, terminals, and sockets have different seek, readiness, buffering, and EOF behavior. read asks for up to a count; short results and EOF depend on the object. A pipe is a stream, not an array with an automatically known final size. [[read]]\n\nA descriptor number may be reused after close. Keeping an old integer in a struct and using it later can target an unrelated newly opened object. This is a handle-lifetime bug analogous to a dangling pointer.\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nTwo processes share a pipe. The producer closes its writer, but the consumer still holds its inherited writer open while reading. Why can EOF fail to arrive? Which exact reference must be removed?\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nAt least one write reference remains: the consumer's unintended inherited writer. EOF depends on all writers closing, not on one designated producer finishing. The consumer must close its writer endpoint. A wait in the producer does not close the consumer's reference.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nIn Lab 05, list descriptor table entries separately from shared kernel objects. Extend the diagram with a duplicate writer and predict what happens when only one duplicate closes.\n"}, {"id": 31, "unit": 9, "title": "Robust I/O loops", "path": "lessons/0031-robust-io-loops.html", "win": "Handle partial progress without losing or duplicating bytes.", "minutes": 30, "search": "Robust I/O loops Handle partial progress without losing or duplicating bytes.  A count is progress\nA positive read or write result is the number of bytes transferred, not a Boolean success flag. A result smaller than requested can be normal. read returning zero indicates EOF for the stream situations used here. A negative result signals an error; inspect errno only when the interface indicates failure. [[read]] [[write]]\n\nFor an exact-length blocking receive, track done. On a positive result, increase it. On zero, decide whether EOF before completion is a valid protocol ending or truncation. On interruption with EINTR, retry according to the operation's contract. Other errors terminate or transfer responsibility to the caller.\n\n Prove the loop invariant\nFor a complete write, the invariant is “bytes [0, done) have been sent exactly once; bytes [done, length) remain.” Each successful call begins at buffer + done and requests length - done. This prevents both duplication and gaps.\n\nIf a positive-length write reports no progress, do not spin forever. Treat it as a defined error condition in this helper. For nonblocking descriptors, EAGAIN means wait for readiness and resume later; repeatedly calling in a busy loop defeats the design. The lab's helper explicitly assumes blocking I/O and reports other errors to its caller.\n\n Bound before subtraction and conversion\nStore capacities in size_t, but syscall results in ssize_t. Test for negative before converting to an unsigned count. A negative ssize_t converted to size_t becomes a huge value, which can corrupt an offset calculation.\n\nKeep every request inside the valid buffer and within platform interface limits. length - done is safe only if done <= length is an invariant. A helper's signature does not excuse a caller from supplying live storage of the promised size.\n\n Test failure paths deliberately\nOrdinary regular-file writes often complete fully, so a successful copy of one small file does not exercise the loop. Lab 06 injects a deterministic sequence of short counts and an interrupt into the actual compiled helper by substituting its write dependency. It separately tests the real compiled copy program with binary data and a closed-pipe error path. Injected-boundary tests and system tests answer different questions.\n\n::: deep Retry is an operation-specific policy\nDo not blindly retry every failed system call. Some calls can have side effects before reporting a failure, and some close/interruption semantics differ by OS. The rule here applies to the documented byte-transfer loop, with explicit progress accounting.\n\nSignals can interrupt blocking operations, while restart settings may cause some operations to resume automatically. Design around the interface's permitted results instead of assuming the handler configuration guarantees a full transfer. [[signal]]\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nA ten-byte send receives return counts 3, -1/EINTR, 2, and 5. List the buffer offset and requested remaining length before each call. Is the error count ever added to progress?\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nOffsets/lengths are (0,10), (3,7), (3,7), and (5,5). The interrupt adds no progress in this sequence; the next attempt resumes at three. Positive results total ten. Adding the signed error result or restarting at zero breaks the invariant.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nReview Lab 06's copy loop and Lab 07's socket helper with the same invariant. Then design an API that distinguishes clean EOF before a new frame from EOF in the middle of a frame; the difference becomes a protocol correctness requirement.\n"}, {"id": 32, "unit": 9, "title": "Buffering and durability", "path": "lessons/0032-buffering-and-durability.html", "win": "Explain which layer acknowledged a write and what remains unproven.", "minutes": 25, "search": "Buffering and durability Explain which layer acknowledged a write and what remains unproven.  Success has a layer\nA call to fprintf may update a user-space buffer without issuing a kernel write immediately. fflush pushes buffered output through the stream's underlying mechanism. A successful write means the kernel accepted the reported bytes under that interface; it does not universally prove persistence after power loss. Synchronization interfaces add a different guarantee. [[write]] [[fsync]]\n\nName the layer in every statement: application record created, stdio buffer accepted, kernel accepted, file state synchronized, or directory update synchronized. “The data was saved” is too ambiguous for a failure investigation.\n\n Buffering changes observations\nA terminal may show line-buffered output promptly, while a redirected stream may be fully buffered. That can make the same program appear to hang or reorder messages relative to an unbuffered channel. An explicit flush is appropriate when the interface requires immediate visibility; flushing after every character can create unnecessary syscall overhead.\n\nAvoid casually mixing stdio operations with raw descriptor operations on the same underlying file. They can have separate buffering state but share offsets through an open-file description. Coordinating them requires the relevant stream rules; separate handles are not independent snapshots. [[dup]]\n\n Atomic replacement is a protocol\nA common local-file update pattern is to create a temporary file in the destination filesystem, write a complete new version, synchronize required file state, rename it into place, then synchronize the containing directory as required for the intended durability contract. Check every operation and define recovery behavior.\n\nA rename can provide atomic namespace replacement under the relevant filesystem rules; atomic visibility is distinct from crash persistence. File and directory synchronization behavior depends on the OS/filesystem/storage stack. The Linux fsync manual explicitly discusses directory entries. This lesson is a reasoning guide, not a universal database recovery proof. [[fsync]]\n\n Failure can arrive late\nStorage errors may be reported on a later write, synchronization, or close rather than at the exact point the application first produced the data. Ignoring cleanup and synchronization errors can therefore lose the only signal that a supposedly successful operation failed.\n\nFor a copy utility, clearly state whether success means “all bytes delivered to the output descriptor” or “durably stored as a named file.” Lab 06 tests byte-transfer correctness and error reporting; it does not claim crash durability.\n\n::: deep Append and transactions are separate\nOpening in append mode can coordinate where each kernel write begins, but a logical record split across several writes may interleave with other writers. A single write's atomicity depends on object type and applicable limits. Pipes have their own atomic-write bound; regular-file behavior is a different contract.\n\nEven a durable sequence of independent writes is not automatically an atomic multi-record transaction. A recovery protocol must determine which partial states are valid and how to detect or repair them. Checksums, commit records, and journaling belong to that larger design.\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nAfter a successful write and before fsync, the machine loses power. What can the application conclude from its earlier return value? What additional namespace issue remains if a new file was created?\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nThe return value established that the reported bytes were accepted by the write interface, not that power-loss persistence was complete. A newly created or renamed pathname involves directory metadata that may need its own synchronization for the intended guarantee. Exact recovery depends on the platform and filesystem; do not infer it from a normal-exit test.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nWrite two success contracts for a file-producing command: one for an ephemeral pipeline and one for a durable configuration replacement. Identify which extra operations and failure checks the second contract requires.\n"}, {"id": 33, "unit": 10, "title": "Endpoints, DNS, and TCP", "path": "lessons/0033-endpoints-dns-and-tcp.html", "win": "Follow a connection from a service name to a byte stream.", "minutes": 25, "search": "Endpoints, DNS, and TCP Follow a connection from a service name to a byte stream.  Names are not connections\nA hostname and service identify a lookup request, not a ready socket. getaddrinfo returns candidate addresses with family and socket information. Iterate candidates deliberately: create a matching socket, attempt the operation, close failed candidates, and release the result list. Use its error-reporting interface rather than assuming every lookup failure is an ordinary errno failure. [[dns]]\n\nAn endpoint includes an address and a port under a transport protocol. A connection uses local and remote endpoints plus protocol/state. DNS, routing, transport establishment, and application behavior can fail independently. “The network is broken” does not identify a layer.\n\n Listen and accept create different responsibilities\nA server creates a socket, binds an address, and listens. accept returns a connected socket; the listening socket remains responsible for new connections. Closing one does not automatically close the other. Each accepted descriptor needs its own ownership and termination path.\n\nLab 07 binds only to IPv4 loopback and lets the OS choose an available port. This is a deliberately small local experiment, not an Internet service. Its address choice keeps setup focused; the general lesson still includes address-family-independent resolution and candidate iteration.\n\n TCP does not deliver messages\nTCP provides an ordered reliable byte stream while the connection is functioning under its protocol rules. Reliability does not mean an application can never see failure, nor does it create record boundaries. A read can return less than one message, several messages' bytes, EOF, or an error. RFC 9293 is the primary protocol specification. [[rfc9293]]\n\nA successful local send does not prove the remote application processed or durably stored the bytes. Application acknowledgments and idempotency rules solve higher-layer questions. Retrying after a disconnected connection can duplicate an operation if the server completed it before the acknowledgment was lost.\n\n Separate clocks and limits\nConnection establishment, idle time, total request time, and processing deadlines are distinct policies. A peer that sends one byte occasionally can defeat a timeout that resets indefinitely on every byte. Bound frame length and overall work even in a teaching service.\n\nOn Linux, socket options, buffering, and transport behavior have detailed contracts in tcp(7). Avoid changing options based on folklore before identifying the workload and latency/throughput objective. [[tcp]]\n\n::: deep Backlog is not application capacity\nA listening backlog concerns queued connection states under OS rules; it is not the worker queue or a guarantee of useful throughput. Accepted connections can each retain buffers and descriptors while waiting. Capacity must include those resources and the application's admission policy.\n\nA remote close can produce EOF in one direction while the other direction still has meaning. TCP supports half-close; protocols must define whether and when a client signals end-of-request by length, delimiter, or closing a direction. Our framed protocol uses explicit lengths, preserving the ability to exchange several frames on one connection.\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nA hostname resolves, but connect fails; another connection succeeds but the application waits forever for a newline that was never sent. Which layers succeeded, and where would you investigate each failure?\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nResolution succeeded in the first case; connection establishment did not, so inspect candidate address, endpoint, routing, listener, and reported error. In the second, transport establishment succeeded; the application framing contract is mismatched. Neither symptom is repaired by assuming DNS was wrong. Collect evidence at the failed boundary.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nDraw the lifetime of Lab 07's listener and one accepted connection. Annotate every descriptor close and the framing state across three writes that split a header and body.\n"}, {"id": 34, "unit": 10, "title": "Parse streams, not writes", "path": "lessons/0034-parse-streams-not-writes.html", "win": "Decode length-prefixed frames across arbitrary input chunk boundaries.", "minutes": 30, "search": "Parse streams, not writes Decode length-prefixed frames across arbitrary input chunk boundaries.  A parser is a state machine\nDefine an original binary protocol: a four-byte unsigned big-endian length followed by exactly that many payload bytes. Permit lengths from zero through 4096. The length excludes the header. This simple format is intentionally not HTTP.\n\nA streaming decoder has header state and payload state. Header state accumulates four bytes. Once complete, decode the length and reject values above the limit before allocating or waiting for that payload. Payload state collects or processes exactly the declared number of bytes. Completing a frame returns to header state, possibly consuming another frame from the same input chunk.\n\nTCP's byte-stream contract requires this independence from read boundaries. [[rfc9293]]\n\n Work a split input\nThe bytes 00 00 00 03 63 61 74 encode payload cat. Suppose input arrives as 00 00, then 00 03 63, then 61 74. After chunk one, header state holds two bytes. After chunk two, the header is complete and payload state holds c with two bytes remaining. Chunk three finishes the frame.\n\nNow concatenate a second complete frame to the final chunk. A correct decoder continues parsing; it does not discard bytes after the first completed payload. EOF with no partial header is a clean end between frames; EOF after two header bytes or in a payload is truncation. The underlying read interface supplies counts and EOF, not the protocol classification. [[read]]\n\n Length checks come before resource commitments\nA malicious or buggy peer can advertise billions of bytes. A bound protects memory and work, but the parser must also check arithmetic such as header length plus payload length when storing combined frames. Set a limit justified by the service, not by the largest representable integer.\n\nZero-length frames are valid in this lab's protocol. They finish immediately after the header, so the state machine must make progress without waiting for a nonexistent payload. Explicitly test this case; otherwise a loop can stall with zero bytes remaining.\n\n Feedback requires adversarial chunking\nLab 07's reference server uses an exact-length blocking helper for one bounded frame at a time. The browser workbench uses incremental parsing to expose arbitrary chunks. Both must produce the same frame sequence for every splitting of the same complete stream. Test all single split points and randomized multi-chunk partitions.\n\n::: deep Protocol ambiguity is a security issue\nHTTP/1.1 has more complicated framing rules, including interactions between length fields and transfer encodings. RFC 9112 defines parsing and message-length rules precisely. When two intermediaries disagree about boundaries, one stream can be interpreted as different request sequences. [[http]]\n\nOur lab removes those complexities to teach a crisp invariant: consumed bytes belong to exactly one header or payload, length is bounded before commitment, and leftover bytes are preserved. Generalizing to HTTP requires implementing the actual standard, not renaming this toy decoder.\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nDecode 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 02 4F 4B. How many frames are present and what are their payload lengths? What changes if EOF arrives before the final byte?\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nThere are two frames: an empty payload and a two-byte payload OK. If the final byte is absent at EOF, the second frame is truncated; the first remains a completed frame. Do not confuse a zero length with EOF, and do not treat the remaining partial payload as a successful message.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nUse the workbench to split a stream inside each header byte, at the header/body boundary, inside a body, and between frames. Then run Lab 07's automated client checks against the compiled server.\n"}, {"id": 35, "unit": 10, "title": "Readiness and backpressure", "path": "lessons/0035-readiness-and-backpressure.html", "win": "Bound output queues and resume partial work after readiness events.", "minutes": 30, "search": "Readiness and backpressure Bound output queues and resume partial work after readiness events.  Readiness is an invitation to try\nA readiness notification indicates that an operation may make progress under the interface's rules. It does not reserve the descriptor's state or promise an entire application message. Use nonblocking I/O so stale readiness cannot unexpectedly block the event loop.\n\nFor a write queue, maintain a buffer and sent offset. Positive writes advance the offset; EAGAIN preserves it and waits for future writable readiness; errors enter a defined connection-close path. Registering perpetual writable interest with no pending data can create a busy loop because sockets are often writable. [[write]]\n\n Edge and level triggering change the loop\nLevel-triggered readiness can continue reporting while the condition holds. With edge-triggered epoll, process available I/O until it reports EAGAIN, or use another explicitly correct strategy. Reading one chunk and waiting for a fresh edge can leave unread data with no new edge to wake you. The Linux epoll manual explains these semantics. [[epoll]]\n\nepoll is Linux-specific; macOS provides different mechanisms such as kqueue, and portable poll has its own interface. The transferable skill is maintaining connection state and progress, not memorizing one event API.\n\n Backpressure protects the whole system\nIf a client reads slowly, generated responses can accumulate. A service that accepts work faster than it can transmit needs a bounded queue and a policy. At a high-water mark, pause intake or decline work; at a lower threshold, resume. Bound per-connection and global resources so many individually small queues cannot exhaust the process.\n\nDropping arbitrary bytes corrupts framing. If overload requires rejection, reject at a valid protocol boundary or close the connection under the protocol's error rules. Specify what the client can retry safely.\n\n A connection owns more than a descriptor\nTrack parser state, pending output, request metadata, deadlines, and closing state. A disconnect can happen in any state. Cleanup must release every owned resource exactly once, and asynchronous callbacks must not retain a dangling reference to an already-reused descriptor number or connection object.\n\nLab 07 is intentionally a one-client-at-a-time blocking reference. Its extension assignment is to design this state machine, not to assume the reference is a scalable production server. The limits of an example are part of its teaching value.\n\n::: deep HTTP adds policy beyond readiness\nPersistent HTTP connections, request bodies, response framing, and early close rules require the standard's semantics in addition to transport progress. A generic event loop does not make a protocol implementation correct. Read the framing and connection-management sections of RFC 9112 before attempting that extension. [[http]]\n\nA slow client is a useful capacity experiment: make it read in small intervals and observe queued bytes, process memory, and response completion. A correct bound should become visible in the queue metric before the machine is exhausted.\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nA server can generate 2 MiB/s for a client consuming 0.5 MiB/s. Ignoring other effects, how fast does backlog grow? With a 6 MiB queue limit, how long can this continue from empty before policy must act?\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nGrowth is 1.5 MiB/s, reaching 6 MiB in four seconds. A queue is temporary storage, not a throughput solution. The service must throttle, reject, reduce output, or disconnect under a defined contract. More buffering only extends the time to saturation.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nSpecify per-connection states and resource bounds for an event-driven extension to Lab 07. Include partial header, partial payload, output pending, EOF, timeout, protocol rejection, and shutdown. Name the invariant that prevents two states from both freeing the connection.\n"}, {"id": 36, "unit": 11, "title": "Races and lock invariants", "path": "lessons/0036-races-and-lock-invariants.html", "win": "Explain a lost update and choose the invariant a lock must protect.", "minutes": 30, "search": "Races and lock invariants Explain a lost update and choose the invariant a lock must protect.  An increment is not a protocol\nIn a simple interleaving model, counter++ contains a read, an addition, and a write. Two workers can both read zero, compute one, and store one, losing an update. The browser scheduler makes that finite sequence visible.\n\nFor an actual C program, conflicting non-atomic accesses without the required synchronization form a data race and invalidate the program's behavior under the language model. It is insufficient to say it merely “sometimes returns one.” Compiler transformations make the real consequence broader than our pedagogical schedule. The draft's execution and atomic rules establish this boundary. [[c]]\n\n Protect an invariant, not a token line\nSuppose a queue has a buffer, head, tail, and count. The invariant relates them: count remains in range, head identifies the next item, tail identifies insertion position, and each queued item appears exactly once. Locking only count++ while modifying the other fields without protection does not preserve that structure.\n\nChoose one mutex that protects the whole relationship. Every path that reads or changes the protected state must follow the same discipline, including error and shutdown paths. A comment such as “mutex protects head/tail/count and buffer slot ownership” is more useful than “thread-safe.”\n\n Locking has its own contract\nAcquire before the protected access and release on every relevant path. A mutex is not generally recursively acquirable unless configured that way; self-deadlock is possible. POSIX thread operations return error codes through their specified return values, often directly rather than through errno.\n\nLocks coordinate access and visibility when used according to the platform/language contracts. They do not extend a freed object's lifetime automatically. The lock object itself must remain alive until nobody can use it.\n\n Challenge the design with tools\nThreadSanitizer can report exercised data races on supported platforms. Build separately from AddressSanitizer and keep workload coverage and tool limitations visible. A clean run is evidence about that execution, not a proof of race freedom. [[tsan]]\n\nOSTEP's lock treatment explores implementation tradeoffs such as spinning versus blocking and fairness. Application code should ordinarily use established primitives rather than writing a lock from a guessed assembly instruction. [[locks]]\n\n::: deep Mutual exclusion is one correctness property\nA program can be race-free yet deadlock, violate ordering requirements, or process an item twice. Correctness needs both safety (“bad things do not happen”) and progress obligations (“required work can eventually proceed under stated assumptions”). A mutex prevents simultaneous critical sections; it does not decide who owns a work item after the critical section ends.\n\nMove long I/O or callbacks outside a lock when the invariant and lifetime allow it. Holding a global mutex across a slow network write can serialize otherwise independent work. Copying a pointer out before unlocking is only safe if the target remains alive through its use.\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nA queue updates head and count under a lock but reads count without locking to decide whether to sleep. Why is this incomplete? Name both a language-level and a protocol-level concern.\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nThe unsynchronized ordinary read can race with updates. Even replacing count with an atomic does not by itself coordinate the check with entering a wait, so a notification can be lost. Correct waiting must protect the predicate and atomically release the mutex while waiting, which the next lesson develops.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nUse the browser scheduler, then annotate Lab 08's queue invariants. Explain why its checking counters are inspected after thread joins rather than sampled unsafely during worker execution.\n"}, {"id": 37, "unit": 11, "title": "Condition variables and queues", "path": "lessons/0037-condition-variables-and-queues.html", "win": "Write predicate-based waits that survive spurious wakeups and shutdown.", "minutes": 30, "search": "Condition variables and queues Write predicate-based waits that survive spurious wakeups and shutdown.  State is the truth; notifications are hints\nA condition variable lets a thread wait for a predicate protected by a mutex. pthread_cond_wait atomically releases the mutex and waits, then reacquires the mutex before returning. Atomicity here closes the check-to-sleep gap relative to other participants following the same locking protocol. [[cond]]\n\nA consumer typically waits while count == 0 && !closed. If items exist, it removes one under the mutex. If the queue is closed and empty, it terminates. The loop is essential: wakeups can be spurious, and another consumer may acquire the mutex first and take the available item.\n\n A bounded queue needs two predicates\nA producer waits while count == capacity && !closed. A consumer waits while count == 0 && !closed. After insertion, a consumer may now proceed; after removal, a producer may now proceed. Separate not-empty and not-full condition variables make these reasons explicit.\n\nSet and test the predicates under the same mutex. Signaling without a state change does not create an item. A signal sent before a thread begins waiting is not stored as a future item count. This is why condition variables and counting semaphores are different abstractions. OSTEP's condition-variable chapter develops the reasoning. [[cv]]\n\n Closing is part of the queue interface\nChoose graceful drain semantics: close prevents new submissions, wakes blocked participants, and lets consumers drain already queued items before returning “done.” A broadcast on close lets every waiter recheck its termination condition. Destroy mutexes and condition variables only after all threads have stopped using them.\n\nAlternative cancellation semantics may discard queued work, but must say who releases each discarded item's resources. In either design, shutdown is a state transition with ownership consequences, not a final free added after the happy path.\n\n Test the awkward schedules\nTest capacity one, more consumers than items, close on an empty queue, close after production, and bursts that fill the queue. Track unique item identities to prove exactly-once consumption. A sum alone can miss a duplicated value paired with a missing value; Lab 08 checks count, sum, and a per-item seen array after joining.\n\n::: deep A wait graph explains hanging tests\nA producer blocked on full needs a consumer to remove an item. A consumer blocked on empty needs an insertion or close. If every worker is waiting, inspect those predicates and the thread expected to make them true. A missing close can strand consumers after the producer exits, even though every mutex operation was individually legal.\n\nKeep the protected critical sections short, but do not split the predicate check from the wait. The lock's scope should match the invariant and transition, not a rule such as “only one line per lock.” Established lock primitives have progress and fairness considerations beyond mutual exclusion. [[locks]]\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nA queue is empty and open. Two consumers wait. The producer inserts one item and broadcasts. Can both consumers remove an item after waking? Trace the mutex acquisition order.\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nNo. One consumer acquires the mutex first, sees the item, and removes it. The second reacquires later, sees empty, and waits again unless close has changed the termination predicate. An if in place of while could let the second remove nonexistent work. The notification did not reserve an item for each waiter.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nRun Lab 08 with capacity one and several worker counts. Explain every shutdown wakeup. Then add an owned heap object as a queued item on paper and specify who frees it on normal completion, failed submission, and cancellation.\n"}, {"id": 38, "unit": 11, "title": "Atomics and publication", "path": "lessons/0038-atomics-and-publication.html", "win": "Distinguish an atomic counter from a safe data-publication protocol.", "minutes": 30, "search": "Atomics and publication Distinguish an atomic counter from a safe data-publication protocol.  Atomicity and ordering answer different questions\nAn atomic increment prevents conflicting updates to that atomic object from losing each other's changes under the atomic contract. Memory ordering determines relationships with other operations. Relaxed atomics can be appropriate for an independent statistic that does not publish other data; they do not automatically make a separate ordinary payload safe to read.\n\nUse <stdatomic.h> and the C atomics interfaces. Whether an atomic type is lock-free depends on the target and type. Atomic does not mean every operation is one instruction or that a whole algorithm is nonblocking. The public draft describes the language model in §§5.1.2.4 and 7.17. [[c]]\n\n State a publication protocol precisely\nAssume one writer, one reader, a payload alive for the entire interaction, and one publication:\n\nc\n/ initially: atomic_bool ready = false; int payload; /\n/ writer /\npayload = 42;\natomic_store_explicit(&ready, true, memory_order_release);\n/ reader /\nif (atomic_load_explicit(&ready, memory_order_acquire)) {\n    / safe to read payload for this one-publication protocol /\n}\n\n\nThe acquire that reads the published true synchronizes with the release. The writer's earlier payload write is then ordered before the reader's subsequent read. If the acquire sees false, it has not received that publication and must not read the uninitialized payload under this protocol.\n\n Do not quietly generalize the example\nResetting the flag and reusing the payload repeatedly introduces new lifetime and ordering questions. A second writer can invalidate the single-writer argument. Freeing the payload while a reader still holds a pointer is a separate reclamation problem. Release/acquire is not a generic permission to read all shared memory forever.\n\nFor complicated ownership or multi-field invariants, a mutex is often clearer. Use the narrowest protocol you can prove and test. Handwritten lock-free structures require an explicit progress argument and memory reclamation design in addition to atomic instructions.\n\n Hardware explanations come after language correctness\nDifferent CPUs implement ordering constraints with different instructions or barriers. A store/load sequence that appears reliable on x86 can fail as a portable source protocol because the compiler and language contract remain relevant. volatile does not replace atomic synchronization.\n\nThe kernel barrier guide is useful advanced reading about ordering mechanisms, but its model and primitives are not the user-space C standard. Keep those domains separate. [[barriers]]\n\n::: deep Compare-exchange does not solve reclamation\nA compare-exchange checks and updates one atomic object under its specified semantics. A pointer value changing A→B→A can make a simple equality check miss intervening history: the ABA problem. If A's storage was freed and reused, dereferencing the “same” address adds an object-lifetime hazard.\n\nHazard pointers, epochs, and other reclamation schemes address different aspects of this problem and require substantial protocols. Do not turn a stack using compare-exchange into a claim of safe lock-free memory management without that additional work. TSan can expose exercised races, but cannot prove an arbitrary lock-free algorithm correct. [[tsan]]\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nWhy is a relaxed atomic ready flag insufficient to publish an ordinary payload? Under the single-publication assumptions, which two operations need release and acquire semantics?\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nRelaxed access coordinates the flag itself without establishing the required synchronization for the payload. The writer's store to the flag uses release; the reader's load that observes it uses acquire. The payload must remain alive and no conflicting later write may violate the protocol. Repeating only the keywords without those assumptions is incomplete.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nChoose between a mutex and an atomic for three tasks: count completed independent jobs, publish an immutable configuration once, and mutate a queue with head/tail/count. Explain the invariant, not merely the expected instruction cost.\n"}, {"id": 39, "unit": 11, "title": "Deadlock and scaling", "path": "lessons/0039-deadlock-and-scaling.html", "win": "Draw a wait cycle and separate correctness from throughput.", "minutes": 25, "search": "Deadlock and scaling Draw a wait cycle and separate correctness from throughput.  Progress needs a separate argument\nRace freedom does not guarantee progress. Two threads can legally hold different locks while each waits for the other's lock. Draw a wait-for graph: thread A points to a resource held by B, and B points back through another resource. A cycle identifies a possible deadlock under this model.\n\nUse a global lock order when operations require multiple locks. If every operation acquires lower-ranked before higher-ranked resources, the specific circular-wait pattern is prevented. Include callbacks and error paths in the rule; a hidden reverse acquisition is still a reverse acquisition. OSTEP explores locking mechanisms and their progress tradeoffs. [[locks]]\n\n Avoid waiting for work that needs your lock\nHolding a queue mutex while joining a worker can deadlock if the worker needs that mutex to finish. Holding a global mutex while making a blocking network write can stall unrelated tasks behind a slow peer. Even without a strict cycle, long critical sections can cause severe queueing.\n\nRestructuring requires ownership reasoning: extract or reserve the needed data under the lock, release it, perform slow work, then commit under the appropriate validation if necessary. Copying an unprotected pointer and hoping its object survives is not a safe shortcut.\n\n More workers can create less useful work\nThroughput initially rises when independent work uses spare cores. It can flatten or decline under contention, bandwidth saturation, scheduling overhead, false sharing, or an inherently serial stage. Latency can worsen before maximum throughput is reached because requests wait in queues.\n\nMeasure completed useful jobs per unit time, response-time distributions, queue depth, resource use, and correctness. A CPU graph at 100% is not evidence that every cycle serves useful work. Spinning can saturate CPU while progress remains poor.\n\n Choose a discriminating observation\nIf the global lock dominates, shorten the critical section or shard state and measure wait/hold behavior. If memory bandwidth dominates, extra workers may add traffic without increasing throughput. If output backpressure dominates, inspect pending bytes and peer consumption. Gregg's USE method turns these into resource questions; profiling examples provide concrete collection techniques on supported Linux environments. [[use]] [[perf]]\n\n::: deep Fairness, starvation, and livelock\nA design can avoid deadlock yet starve a participant indefinitely under its scheduling assumptions. Livelock means participants keep changing state without completing useful work. Random retries can reduce collisions statistically, but do not automatically establish a strong progress guarantee.\n\nSpecify the guarantee you actually need: eventual shutdown, bounded queue size, no permanently stranded items, or a particular fairness property. The platform's mutex and condition-variable contracts may not promise the exact FIFO wakeup order you imagined. Design correctness without relying on accidental scheduler behavior.\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nA controller holds the queue mutex and calls join on consumers. The consumers wake on close but must reacquire that mutex to observe the closed state. Explain the wait cycle and a corrected shutdown order.\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nThe controller waits for consumers to terminate while retaining the mutex they need to progress. Set closed and broadcast under the mutex, release the mutex, then join workers, and only afterward destroy synchronization objects. This sequence lets awakened workers observe the predicate and exit. Every resource must remain alive until its last use.\n:::\n\n Transfer\nWrite a scaling investigation for Lab 08 with one, two, four, and more workers where available. Distinguish a scheduling-dependent result from a correctness assertion. Use the same workload and report the queue's capacity as part of the experiment.\n"}, {"id": 40, "unit": 12, "title": "The whole-system investigation", "path": "lessons/0040-the-whole-system-investigation.html", "win": "Defend a systems diagnosis with contracts, artifacts, and reproducible evidence.", "minutes": 30, "search": "The whole-system investigation Defend a systems diagnosis with contracts, artifacts, and reproducible evidence.  Choose one system and follow its boundaries\nThe capstone is a bounded loopback frame service evolved from Labs 04, 06, 07, and 08. It receives a four-byte length header, validates a bounded body, performs a deterministic transformation, and returns a framed result. You will add an investigation harness and one justified improvement rather than build a giant application.\n\nStart with the blocking reference. Establish correct behavior for ordinary, empty, maximum-length, fragmented, oversized, and truncated input. The reference intentionally has limited concurrency; replacing it with a worker pool is an extension that requires explicit queue and shutdown contracts. Do not call the service HTTP: RFC 9112 specifies a different protocol. [[http]]\n\n Build a contract ledger\nAt the representation boundary, specify header width, unsigned range, and byte order. At the C boundary, specify buffer capacity, initialized length, and ownership. At the process boundary, specify descriptor ownership and exit status. At the concurrency boundary, specify queue invariants and publication. At the performance boundary, specify useful work and workload distribution.\n\nA broken system often has individually plausible components with incompatible contracts. The ledger makes those mismatches inspectable before they produce a mysterious symptom.\n\n Make a claim that can lose\nChoose one target: a parser that mishandles fragmented input, an unbounded queue, a memory lifetime error, a traversal hotspot, or a lock-held I/O path. Write symptom, competing hypotheses, predicted observations, and an intervention.\n\nExamples of useful evidence include a failing protocol test, sanitizer report, annotated assembly, descriptor table, queue-depth series, or repeated timing samples. Select evidence for the question rather than collecting every metric available. Gregg's resource-oriented method and profiling examples provide practitioner models for this discipline. [[use]] [[perf]]\n\n Separate a repair from an improvement\nA repair restores the intended contract. An optimization preserves the contract while improving a measured property for a defined workload. You may do both, but report them separately. Keep the baseline and changed implementation, the same oracle, and raw samples.\n\nAfter a memory repair, rerun the original reproducer and relevant boundary inputs with supported instrumentation. ASan provides concrete evidence for exercised accesses, not a blanket proof of lifetime correctness. Explain the invariant that now prevents the invalid operation. [[asan]]\n\n::: deep Write for a skeptical future reader\nYour report should let someone reproduce the result without this conversation. Include OS and architecture, tool versions, build flags, exact commands, workload generation, expected outputs, raw observations, and limitations. Link observations to claims: “RSS plateaued under this workload” is stronger and narrower than “there are no memory problems.”\n\nFor performance, include correctness and tail behavior. A service with excellent average throughput but unbounded slow-client buffering can still fail its mission. For concurrency, include close/drain behavior and prove no waiter is stranded. For protocols, include adversarial chunk boundaries rather than only polite clients.\n:::\n\n Retrieve before looking\nA profile shows most sampled CPU time in parsing, but wall time is dominated by waiting for a slow reader. Which metric should guide a latency fix? What would you measure next?\n\n::: solution Worked feedback\nWall-clock response time is the target if the mission is latency. An on-CPU profile describes where execution time goes while running; it does not account for all waiting. Measure pending output, peer consumption, blocking/readiness state, and end-to-end timing. Then test a backpressure/deadline policy. Making parsing faster may barely change the user's wait.\n:::\n\n Demonstrate the mission\nComplete Lab 09's rubric and close the source-to-hardware loop. Explain one request as encoded bytes, C operations, compiled instructions, memory accesses, kernel I/O, and synchronized ownership. You are ready to extend the course when you can defend that account and identify what remains uncertain—not when every page has a checkmark.\n"}];
